     1	# Tessera V1 service contract and failure model (outside view)
     2	
     3	> **Status: OWNER DRAFT, 2026-10-24** (Claude Opus 5.5). At the author's request, this states what Tessera V1
     4	> promises the people who use it and rely on it, from outside. It names no internal component. It does not change
     5	> the registered claim (A9.1, A10) or `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md`, which covers what a receipt establishes; where they
     6	> differ, they win. Clause ids are never reused: a retired id stays retired, and a changed clause gets a new id.
     7	> **Undecided** marks a promise V1 has not yet made. Each one is a decision the service owes its users before it
     8	> ships.
     9	
    10	## Parties
    11	
    12	- **Client:** submits content and receives a receipt.
    13	- **Holder:** keeps a receipt. Often the client, but not always.
    14	- **Adjudicator:** decides whether to rely on a receipt, possibly long after issue, possibly after Tessera is gone.
    15	- **Operator:** runs the service for the domain `wamason.com`.
    16	
    17	## 1. Promises to the client
    18	
    19	- **S1.1 Submit.** The client sends content bytes, up to a stated size limit. The service accepts the request or
    20	  refuses it with a stated reason. *Undecided:* the size limit offered to clients (the format allows 16 MiB).
    21	- **S1.2 No partial receipts.** The service delivers either a complete receipt or nothing. It never delivers a
    22	  receipt that would fail verification because something is missing.
    23	- **S1.3 Delivery time.** A receipt cannot be complete until the public chain has confirmed it, so delivery is
    24	  delayed by design. *Undecided:* the promised bound. In the two real runs a verified receipt took 56 minutes and 2 hours 12 minutes
    25	  from signing. That excludes a one-time wait of about an hour at the start of each issuance window. Is the
    26	  receipt pushed to the client, or collected by request id?
    27	- **S1.4 Refusal.** A refusal is explicit and names its reason. Refused content is not receipted later without a
    28	  new request. Expected reasons: content too large, service outside its issuance window, signing unavailable,
    29	  service closing.
    30	- **S1.5 Resubmission.** *Undecided:* does the same content, submitted twice, get one receipt or two? Two valid
    31	  receipts for one content are possible and harmless to what a receipt establishes (`V1-CONTRACT.md` §3). But a
    32	  client may expect idempotence.
    33	- **S1.6 Content exposure.** The service sees the content bytes, and the receipt contains them. A client who wants
    34	  secrecy submits a digest of the secret, not the secret itself. *Undecided:* whether the service keeps content
    35	  after delivery, and for how long.
    36	- **S1.7 Cost.** *Undecided.* A9.1 promises receipts "at a stated cost and latency"; neither is stated yet.
    37	- **S1.8 Availability.** *Undecided:* no availability target is offered. An outage delays or refuses new receipts.
    38	  It never affects receipts already delivered (A1.3).
    39	
    40	## 2. Promises to the adjudicator
    41	
    42	- **A1.1 What a receipt establishes** is `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md` §2, under its conditions, with its limits (§3).
    43	  Neither is repeated here.
    44	- **A1.2 Authority records stay published.** While the service operates, the operator keeps the DNS record for the
    45	  service's keys and the git repository holding its key assertion public and unchanged for issued windows.
    46	  Publication is an issuer duty. The verifier does not check it.
    47	- **A1.3 No dependence on the service.** Checking a receipt needs the receipt, an authenticated verifier release and
    48	  its trust file. It needs nothing the operator runs.
    49	- **A1.4 Verifier releases.** The operator publishes verifier releases, signed by a production release key whose
    50	  fingerprint is published through at least two channels independent of the operational environment.
    51	  *Not yet available:* production releases start at the first public verifier release (A10.4.2).
    52	- **A1.5 Exit.** If the service stops, receipts already issued stay checkable (A1.3). *Undecided:* what the
    53	  operator promises at exit, such as notice, a final verifier release, or archiving the authority records.
    54	- **A1.6 Capture notice.** V1 detects no capture of its authority records or keys. *Undecided:* whether the
    55	  operator promises to announce a known capture, and through which channel.
    56	
    57	## 3. Limits of the V1 service
    58	
    59	- **L1.1 One issuance window.** V1 is registered for one issuance window of at most 90 days. Successor windows need a
    60	  separately registered rotation (A9.6), and none exists yet. **Without one, V1 issues receipts for at most 90 days.**
    61	- **L1.2 One controller.** One operator controls both signing keys. V1 claims no separation of control.
    62	- **L1.3 Single domain.** Receipts name the operator's service, not the client. V1 identifies no submitter.
    63	
    64	## 4. Failure model (as seen from outside)
    65	
    66	| # | What happens | What the client sees | What the adjudicator can still rely on |
    67	|---|---|---|---|
    68	| F1.1 | Service temporarily down | Requests refused or delayed (S1.8) | Every receipt already issued |
    69	| F1.2 | Service gone for good | No new receipts | Every receipt already issued, with the verifier release and trust file the holder kept (A1.3) |
    70	| F1.3 | Public chain slow | Later delivery (S1.3) | Unchanged |
    71	| F1.4 | Public chain reorganizes fewer than 6 blocks | Possibly later delivery | Unchanged |
    72	| F1.5 | Public chain reorganizes 6 or more blocks | Not covered | Not covered (`V1-CONTRACT.md` §3) |
    73	| F1.6 | One signing path unavailable | Refusal (S1.4), never a partial receipt (S1.2) | Unchanged |
    74	| F1.7 | Issuance window ends | Refusal (S1.4); service ends unless a rotation is registered (L1.1) | Every receipt already issued |
    75	| F1.8 | The operator's issuing account is captured | Nothing visible | **Receipts made by the captor pass** (`V1-CONTRACT.md` §4) |
    76	| F1.9 | Both authority records are captured | Nothing visible | Honest receipts stay protected; **receipts under the captor's own keys pass** |
    77	| F1.10 | Release key stolen | Nothing visible | Only holders who installed no later release are unaffected |
    78	| F1.11 | A request is retried, or answered late | Possibly two valid receipts (S1.5) | Each one, at its own T |
    79	| F1.12 | Operator clock wrong | Refusal near the window edges | Unchanged: chain time governs |
    80	
    81	## 5. What this draft found
    82	
    83	Writing the outside view surfaced decisions V1 has never made:
    84	- S1.3, S1.7: the delivery bound and the cost, both promised by A9.1 and stated nowhere.
    85	- S1.5: whether resubmission is idempotent.
    86	- S1.6: whether content is retained.
    87	- A1.5, A1.6: exit and capture-notice promises.
    88	- **L1.1: V1's issuance life is at most 90 days**, because no rotation is registered. Any client relying on the
    89	  service past one window needs to know that.

