     3	## 2026-10-23 — Row 2: CUT
     4	
     5	**AUTHOR RULING (Tony).** In the review conversation, the author stated:
     6	
     7	> With that said, I think the decision for Row 2 is Cut, with the explanation that you recommended.
     8	
     9	The author chooses to cut the service-key listing (A10.4.1) from V1.
    10	This narrows the option (e) adopted on 2026-10-18; it is an author scope
    11	decision, not an invocation of the schedule-slip cut order. It does not
    12	retroactively authorize the owner's 2026-10-19 cut.
    13	
    14	**Rationale (conversation summary).** Keep V1's complexity manageable;
    15	accept the stated dual-authority-capture risk for early receipts; preserve
    16	the option to protect future issuance. The author expects low initial use
    17	and judges the risk from the system's existing complexity to be high.
    18	This decision stands independently of the exit deadline.
    19	
    20	**Accepted limitation.** An attacker controlling both DNS and git authority
    21	channels can issue passing receipts under its own keys, even while the
    22	original service keys remain uncompromised. Protection of honest signed
    23	material remains subject to its registered assumptions. Release-key custody
    24	(A10.4.2) remains required.
    25	
    26	**Upgrade boundary.** A later version may add the listing for future
    27	issuance. A listing created afterward cannot establish the required
    28	pre-receipt commitment for earlier receipts. This is a known V1 limitation;
    29	adding the mechanism later is an option, not an obligation.
    30	
    31	This entry records Row 2 only. It neither accepts other E9 rows nor asserts
    32	that dependent documents and freeze artifacts have been reconciled.
