docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:21:**Exact fix:** State the complete exception: V1 verifies the named historical channel assertions and their bindings, but does not determine whether effective revocation defeats authorization at the declared time or anywhere in the temporal window. Amend both affected P6 conditions for this profile and the corresponding prereg §4.4 interpretation. Change C4/headline to “the named channels authenticated publication of these keys for D, at the specified evidence times; effective revocation is not evaluated.” If retaining the stronger authorization claim, supply authenticated status evidence now. Add a case where revocation precedes declared issuance yet the old publication evidence remains cryptographically valid. A `VALID_STRICT` V1 report must expose its profile-specific reduced meaning prominently.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:## 3. The proposed “later capture does not repudiate V1 receipts” test fails its own promise and contradicts Amendment 4
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:31:**Exact fix:** Choose and name one of two contracts. (a) Preserve an immutable, versioned V1 base evaluation under the same bundle, profile, policy and trust configuration; report later LTV findings in a separate assessment that may contradict authorization without rewriting the base report. Or (b) promise only that missing LTV capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt, and explicitly permit later authenticated adverse evidence to change the stronger assessment. Do not call (b) non-repudiation. Define stable fields, evidence authenticity/freshness, and the interaction with historical authorization. Add vectors for revocation before declared time, between anchor and declared in both orders, after both, absent status, conflicting status, and unauthenticated/backdated status. Preserve the missing-capture broken companion. Label `G_v1` regression evidence, not a proof of the universal contract.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:33:## 4. C4 promises repository publication that the archived git evidence cannot establish
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:37:The prototype report says plainly that an unpushed commit gives identical archived bytes and that nothing in the evidence proves public publication (`prototype/PHASE3-REPORT.md:190-199`). A signature plus OTS establishes a signed commitment and an existed-by bound under the repository trust key. It does not establish distribution through a public repository at issuance. Git and DNSSEC also require their own historical validity/temporal relationships; an author-chosen commit date is not independent time evidence.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:39:There is an additional implementation gap in the proposed two-hour completion test: `prototype/tessera_proto/real/gitchannel.py:125-134` verifies object IDs and the commit-to-root-tree link, but does not verify every tree-entry link down to the alleged key-publication blob. The collector builds a path from local git; an offline verifier must check the adversarially supplied path itself. Passing this function is insufficient evidence that the signed commit contains the claimed publication.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:41:**Exact fix:** Describe the git channel as an authenticated, time-anchored key assertion rooted in the distributed repository key. If actual public publication is part of V1, define separate publication evidence and its trust assumption. Require complete commit→tree→subtree→blob path verification, exact extraction/comparison of D and the manifest/key set, signature verification, and binding to the commit's OTS proof. Add a detached/substituted-blob negative case. Do not use `gitchannel.py`'s current successful return as the complete channel acceptance criterion.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:49:**Exact fix:** Split M8. Put historical trust-configuration identity, archived validation material, owner-name/key binding and authenticated snapshot-time/window checks under NEEDED-FOR-V1, C4/C6. Put continuing status capture/freshness and renewal under DESIGN-LEVEL. Define how the snapshot and manifest are anchored, including any additional proofs and temporal checks; distinguish author-evidence proofs from the receipt's own proof. Verify actual issuer-domain snapshots offline. Specify the trust configuration independently of adversarial bundle content; naming it does not mean blindly trusting a bundle-supplied store.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:59:## 7. C5 and the latency statement conflate chain-time predicates with wall-clock guarantees
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:63:Adopted A2.1 says `confirmed_at` is the designated block-header timestamp, not the wall-clock instant depth k was reached (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:185-206`). Header timestamps can be non-monotonic. C5's “confirmed ... within δ” suppresses this distinction. The observed 8.6–12.6-hour spacing between attestations is not a latency distribution. δ=72h bounds the acceptance predicate of one attempt; retries, propagation, signer availability and calendars do not yield an end-to-end 72-hour wall-clock SLA. The profile also omits the operational slack parameter S that the registered wall-clock attempt rule uses (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:106-135`).
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:**Exact fix:** Say “the authenticated anchor and k-th confirmation header timestamps satisfy the registered three-conjunct consistency predicate.” State Bitcoin timestamp skew, canonical-history/checkpoint assumptions and deeper-than-k reorganization residual beside C5. Include S, its operational clock role and timeout/retry accounting in the ratified profile. Publish measured service latency separately from chain-time acceptance and a bounded refusal deadline. Replace “bounded by δ” with that distinction; say hours are expected, not established as an upper service bound by these observations.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:## 11. Strict-only support is reasonable; the blanket out-of-profile verdict silently changes P4 precedence
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:95:Rejecting unsupported wrappers or waiver requests is an honest capability restriction. It does not justify overwriting a definite failed required check with `UNVERIFIABLE` whenever some other field is unsupported. Adopted A4.2 requires `INVALID` when one required check fails and another is unperformable (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-4.md:88-109`). “Anything else gets UNVERIFIABLE” neither defines profile-dispatch scope nor preserves that rule. Missing required signatures and missing required authority evidence also have different registered meanings; d2/d3 cannot simply be lumped together without inspecting their actual inputs.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:97:**Exact fix:** Define the outer profile-dispatch result separately from in-profile required-check aggregation. For recognized V1 objects, preserve FAIL-over-UNPERFORMABLE precedence. For unsupported profiles/layers, return a scoped unsupported assessment without asserting their validity. Distinguish an unsupported verification request from a fresh strict request over the same intact bundle. Add a mixed failed/unsupported case and missing-signature versus missing-evidence cases to E6a. If changing P4 itself, list a second explicit property weakening instead of claiming only P6 changes.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:103:A typed result makes distinctions expressible and testable; it does not by itself make a relying party's decision safe. In case e the proposed verifier deliberately accepts the bytes while not evaluating a fact that defeats authorization in the original construction. Telling the user to “check the issuer's revocation publication” introduces a current external source precisely where the offline claim promises no network. That advice may also be impossible when Tessera has disappeared. The prototype tests expose interpretation errors; they do not already establish non-expert success.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:105:**Exact fix:** Define the safe decision for each scenario and each transaction question. For e: the base bytes/signature/publication/time checks pass; historical non-revocation is **not established**, so a transaction requiring it must not rely on that result alone. Describe external status checking as an optional stronger workflow, with authenticated evidence requirements and an explicit unavailable outcome. Phrase C7 as the December acceptance criterion until tested. Name the non-expert integrator, documentation-only conditions and scoring rule. A fresh model is useful additional evidence; replacing the scheduled non-expert integrator with it changes the adopted success proposal and must be explicitly ruled.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:107:## 13. Changing expectations is insufficient: the ten scenario fixtures do not fit the proposed profile
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:111:The actual a fixture includes a TLR; d1 uses that complete bundle under a degraded policy; d3 is an attacker clone with one signing key/one channel and its own TLR; f's original expected standing is also `ESTABLISHED` (`prototype/consumers/scenarios.py:72-117`). Under literal “no TLR, no waivers” the unchanged a/d1 bundles cannot simultaneously be V1 `VALID_STRICT`. Removing only a/e/h expectations misses these contradictions. The original wrapper attacks should remain unsupported-profile tests, not be erased. These prototype expectations are previously observed test artifacts, not registered original product obligations of the same kind as the other thirteen rows.
docs/reviews/v1-derivation/CODEX-REVIEW.md:113:**Exact fix:** Register a V1 scenario mapping before V1 runs that gives, for every a,b,c,d1,d2,d3,e,f,g,h case: exact fixture transformation or preserved bytes, profile, verification request, expected base verdict, standing/status fields and safe decision. For d1 explicitly refuse the waiver request and separately evaluate a strict request. Preserve the original prototype scenarios/results as history. Include the real-mechanism mirror/authority attacks and model-derived vectors in the larger conformance set without pretending they prove universal user comprehension. Put the prospective test registration in its own category in the omission inventory.
