docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:3:2026-10-13. **Artifact: PROPOSED, unsigned A9; author read/sign next.** Reviewed A9 in full, the preserved pre-revision text and `delta.diff`, both custody/profile CODEX reviews and dispositions, V1–V2 compatibility analysis, ENCODING-V1's governing signature, DNS/window and git sections, and the earlier registered sentences identified below. Current profile wording was checked for reconciliation. Only this review file was edited.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:11:**Location:** A9:296–303; A9.4:258–263; opening adversary/boundaries; source profile F2 and disposition.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:15:The extension bounds eligible **snapshot chain time** by signing-end plus the configured maximum lifetime. A9 already discloses that A2.1 gives no finite backward-skew bound for T; the same issue must not disappear for the snapshot anchor. Thus there is no established finite UTC exposure bound. Historical KSK safety remains assumed, not evaluated. Moreover, a maximum observed in archived captures is a policy value, not proof of the maximum over all signatures the root ever issued. The source disposition is candid about using observed windows; A9's universal “maximum ... it made” is stronger.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:27:The delta contains the requested phrases “per-algorithm cutoff compared with T” and “reported separately”. It preserves the same-input contract and the C7 missing-capture companion. But it says only that T *before* a cutoff preserves the V1 verdict. At equality or after it, it is unclear whether the V1 verdict changes, despite the next sentence saying historical-security results never replace that verdict. Authentication of the cutoff is stated in the historical-security paragraph but omitted from the new policy rule. Key-theft cutoffs must remain separate too. A cutoff is not a demonstration that security held earlier, especially with A2.1 uncertainty.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:45:The policy signer, credential boundary, dedicated use, hybrid deadline and limited independence text faithfully implement custody F1/F2/F4. C3 still honestly allows queue-based misuse of both slots by an AWS takeover; external policy prevents bypassing the clock check, not adversarial current-time issuance. No new independent approval or control is claimed.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:47:However, row 15 explicitly says “The ‘local GPG’ placement is kept”, and the later inventory calls that placement unchanged. A9.6 now permits an HSM and policy signer at another cloud provider. That replaces the earlier registered placement, even though the fingerprint scheme and rule O stay unchanged. A first-time reader encounters two incompatible deployment requirements.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:51:> The registered local-GPG placement is replaced for V1 by A9.6's slot-2 custody alternatives, both through the external policy signer; rule O and the gpg fingerprint scheme are retained.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:55:**Unfinished source reconciliation:** ENCODING-V1:441 still says `rule O (P-256, hardware token; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA)`, despite custody disposition saying this repair was delegated. The exact source-review replacement remains appropriate: `rule O (OpenPGP P-256; slot-2 custody per A9.6; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA)`. A9 binds this file at signing, so delegation is not evidence that the reconciliation is done. This is a prose custody correction, not a signature-byte or vector change.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:77:Sequential replacement fits the frozen **prose** verifier rules: each receipt supplies its own historical snapshot/commit, the same owner/path can contain a different pair of fingerprints, and each individual window satisfies the ≤90-day and C4 checks. There is no current-record lookup or successor relation to check. This is structural compatibility, not a tested frozen service verifier or P10/capstone result. The verifier also does not check cross-receipt non-overlap. At the shared endpoint, both inclusive windows can contain that instant; `next.not-before >= old.not-after` in the source is not literal non-overlap under ENC's inclusive rules.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:83:> This amendment registers the first issuance window only; it does not yet authorize successor issuance. A future sequential-replacement registration may retain the frozen V1 byte/check profile: the same DNS owner and git path, new keys, and a new window with next.not-before > previous.not-after (both endpoints are inclusive). Each receipt carries its own historical snapshot and commit. Multiple TXT records at the required owner fail DNS_RRSET; a substituted per-key owner name fails DNS_OWNER. The V1 verifier checks each receipt's window, not non-overlap between receipts. Before successor issuance, the owner records a dated rotation registration naming the two-window positive/negative vectors and the model, checked property and result establishing the intended issuance invariant. This future rotation evidence is not added to the 30 Oct first-window gate. Without that registration, issuance stops at the first window's end.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:87:## F6 — MEDIUM: git-format policy has no evidence schema or verdict contract, and the revised trust policy still conflicts with the profile
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:89:**Location:** A9:291–295; ENCODING-V1 §7.2 and out-of-scope list; V1-PROFILE §2.1/§5; compatibility §2.9; profile disposition F2.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:93:But “git evidence names its object format” does not define a member name/type, missing-field behavior, unsupported-format verdict, or exact stamp bytes. ENCODING-V1 does **not** define the git evidence container. §7.2 fixes the signed-commit target and describes lowercase id plus LF as a repository convention. The field belongs in the bundle's git-evidence schema when V1-PROFILE §2.1/G-26's container/member encoding is settled, with the normative check in its git-channel section. Putting it into the closed signed common schema or the 255-byte key-publication grammar would change P8, unnecessarily.
docs/reviews/a9-final-delta/CODEX-REVIEW.md:97:> The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member object_format as the string "sha1". It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING; an unsupported value gives UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT. The verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
