docs/reviews/anchor-signatures/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:   **Evidence:** docs/V1-DERIVATION-DRAFT.md:55 authenticates K for D at authority evidence times, :93 describes the snapshot-time rule; formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:297-305 defines a manifest containing keys/issuer/algorithm, :325-330 gives the snapshot a separate anchor, and :354-360 defines a key-publication record with no receipt digest.
docs/reviews/anchor-signatures/CODEX-REVIEW.md:27:   Reject the proposed rebuttal that DNSSEC snapshot anchoring or the signed, anchored git assertion closes this attack. An attacker can reuse authentic contemporary evidence that the actual Tessera keys were authorized for D. The commit→tree→blob checks authenticate the assertion, not the later attestation signatures. The snapshot's RRSIG windows and anchor constrain that snapshot, not the receipt signatures. The manifest hash, slot fingerprints, profile, algorithm and declared time can all be correctly pre-staged. No specified authority statement commits to this receipt's signatures or its issuance event.
docs/reviews/anchor-signatures/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:   **Evidence:** docs/V1-DERIVATION-DRAFT.md:54 says nothing about key lifecycle after T; :56 gives chain-time, presentation and reorganization residuals; :65-66 specifies offline trust configuration and no renewal/revocation evaluation. formal/p8/PROOF.md:60-70 separates hash binding from signature assumptions. docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md:473-482 explicitly says OTS does not fill a signing gap and keeps PQ a production gate.
docs/reviews/anchor-signatures/CODEX-REVIEW.md:67:   Correct conditional statement: an exact signature demonstrably existing before signing capability became available to the adversary cannot be manufactured afterward and substituted into that old receipt while the commitment and temporal evidence remain secure. Historical authenticity additionally requires the appropriate signing/key-use assumptions before that bound, authentic historical key authority, and no relevant pre-bound compromise. A later P-256 break need not invalidate such historical evidence by itself. It does not cure earlier theft, unauthorized pre-T signing, misissuance, or whole-author-sphere capture. It does not prove the issuer itself obtained the OTS stamp or uniquely issued the bundle.
docs/reviews/anchor-signatures/CODEX-REVIEW.md:79:   Compare the trusted existence upper bound with the cutoff, accounting for the registered chain-time uncertainty: a Bitcoin header timestamp is not an exact external UTC clock. If there is no defensible time bound strictly before the cutoff, the historical conclusion is not established. Because T is the earliest attestation in the presented proof, pruning away an earlier attestation can remove this evidence; it cannot legitimately move a new signature into the past. Use only verified presented attestations and fail to establish the historical conclusion when the needed earlier proof is absent.
