docs/reviews/amendment-9/CODEX-REVIEW.md:133:- **Scenario rows:** a/g, b, c, d1/d2/d3, e/e′, f, h and i/i′ were all checked. INVALID precedence in d3 is coherent for a V1-dispatched missing-signature case despite unavailable authority evidence. e/e′ deliberately do not evaluate revocation. i/i′ distinguish timestamped contract bytes from the relying party’s party-signature check. The initial “strict request in every row” should allow d1’s explicitly described preliminary degraded request. A9.4’s missing-capture test and the larger E10 suite remain separate from comprehension testing.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/CODEX-REVIEW.md:137:- **A9.5:** exact signature inclusion, sign–verify–freeze–stamp, one composite receipt anchor, exact committed-byte verification and signed-commit-id anchoring match revised ENCODING-V1/PROOF. No frame-only fallback is allowed. The existential KSK rule correctly avoids treating a published nonsigning KSK as a failure; validity still requires the other DNSSEC chain/time checks. The spike’s re-capture is real rollover evidence, but unstamped and not a complete V1 receipt.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/CODEX-REVIEW.md:138:- **A9.6:** guarantee 1 is appropriately conditional and does not establish historical honesty, issuer-obtained stamping or uniqueness. Interpret “by T” with C5’s chain-time uncertainty, not as exact UTC. Guarantee 2 needs finding 2. Hybrid/fallback, custody and exit are explicit owner choices; findings 4/5/8 reconcile their detail without adding a new service.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/CODEX-REVIEW.md:139:- **A9.7:** every row was checked above; exact ranges and omissions need repair. “Named and not changed” is coherent when scoped to the later product/general profile, not an assertion that unresolved H0 has been discharged for V1.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/CODEX-REVIEW.md:148:The prose proof distinguishes codec injectivity, SHA-256 binding, signature unforgeability and temporal composition; this review does not collapse those into discharge. No new ProVerif/TLA+ run, AWS/hardware integration, human relying-party test or dead-service demonstration was performed. The classical spike lacked the key TXT publication and git channel; the PQ spike was a throwaway feasibility experiment. Their green portions do not establish C1–C7 end to end. The remaining registered freeze/build tests must supply that evidence.
