docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:1:# Codex review — V1 profile and steps 11–12
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:11:Location: `prototype/spike/verify_offline.py:119–127`, `Ledger.status:37–42`. Governing rule: A9:285–289, profile §5.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:15:Reproduced without editing fixtures: load `out/dns-snapshot.json`; copy its root DNSKEY RRSIG with `key_tag=38696` (the unchanged cryptographic signature is invalid for that key); add it to the root RRSIG set; put KSK-2024 first in a copied trust configuration and move its signing start to 1 October so both candidates are eligible. Evaluate at the capture time. The rows are KSK-2024 FAIL followed by KSK-2017 PASS; the group is FAIL. The original authentic KSK-2017 signature remains intact. This is an avoidable false reject of an otherwise valid root RRset, and contradicts A9.5. A 2106 verifier would reproduce it with the same inputs.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:21:Location: profile:202–208; `spike/trust-config.json`, KSK-2017 signing end; verifier:100–116.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:The owner's exact cutoff is an additional acceptance policy, not a fact established by the observed change of signer. IANA confirms the rollover date and cessation of KSK-2017 signing on 11 October, but does not establish an end-of-day cutoff for validation of archived signatures. See [IANA rollover information](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/files) and [IANA rollover announcement](https://www.iana.org/news/2026/root-zone-begins-using-new-ksk-2026). The report's new capture was actually taken on 12 October; the RRSIG inception is not proof of the exact instant every cache stopped returning old signed sets.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:An authentic root DNSKEY set signed by KSK-2017 before rollover remains cryptographically valid through its RRSIG expiry. An honest cached root set or snapshot captured before retirement can be OTS-anchored after the proposed cutoff. Snapshot anchoring is asynchronous; DD:94 allows up to δ, subject to all actual RRSIG windows. A fresh lower chain can coexist with that cached root set. Saying honest snapshots after 11 October never carry KSK-2017 signatures excludes this ordinary case without evidence.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:35:Location: verifier:104–117; profile:209–213.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:49:Decision (4) is sound: without the snapshot's own authenticated time, no receipt time, capture clock or present clock can substitute. Missing evidence is UNVERIFIABLE unless another required check actually fails. The default no-usable-anchor path makes that distinction correctly; the file/message loading paths do not. P8 requires a separate snapshot envelope/proof; A9 C4 evaluates at its time.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:55:Location: verifier:81–89, 218–224, 252–255; profile §7.3.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:57:`dns.dnssec.validate` accepts when an appropriate signature validates, but `val()` then appends every signature in the candidate RRset to `used`. An extra expired, not-yet-valid or invalid signature can therefore narrow the joint window and trigger a FAIL despite a valid signature path. This is another avoidable false reject, especially around a rollover. It also misstates the evidence underlying the reported joint window. The profile's “every RRSIG window in the chain” needs to mean the signatures actually selected to authenticate the chain, not every offered alternative.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:61:### F6 — MEDIUM: snapshot tolerance is checked against capture time rather than the receipt's signed declared time
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:63:Location: verifier:218–221, 241–256; profile §7.3; DD:94.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:The implementation sets `snap_declared = snap['captured_end']` and compares the snapshot anchor to that. It never checks the snapshot anchor against the receipt's signed `declared_issue_time`. DD and the profile require the snapshot anchor within `[declared − ε, declared + δ]`; in the receipt context “declared” is the signed issue time, and neither source defines a second snapshot declaration that replaces it. A historical snapshot with a locally satisfactory capture/anchor pair can be supplied alongside a much later receipt without this check detecting the mismatch. Whether every other authority rule could permit a complete unsafe acceptance remains unresolved; this is an assigned C4 temporal check that is missing, not a demonstrated complete forgery.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:67:**Proposed fix:** specify exactly which declared time this predicate means. If it is receipt issue time, pass `declared` to this required check; retain capture-to-anchor latency as a separate observation or explicitly registered additional check. If reusable snapshot evidence is intended, register its age relation to the receipt rather than silently replacing the existing predicate. Add independently valid receipt/snapshot proofs whose times are far apart.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:69:### F7 — MEDIUM: step 11 verifies linkage, but does not exercise the selected profile contract
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:71:Location: `gitchannel.py:193–230`; `tests/test_gitpath.py:10`; profile §6.3.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:77:**Proposed fix:** keep the helper general if useful, but add an instrument composition that fixes the profile path, verifies the signature over payload derived from the same raw commit, verifies its stamp, compares raw DNS/blob bytes and gates acceptance on signed issuer == pinned D. Test a correct record only at the wrong path, an alternate issuer under the same repository key, mismatched DNS/git values, unsigned commit and unstamped commit. Make the coverage limitation explicit until that composition exists.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:79:### F8 — LOW: the profile claims extraction-only provenance while making new decisions
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:81:Location: profile:12–16, §5, §6.3.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:83:“It does not decide anything new” is inconsistent with the dated new owner decisions. Those decisions are clearly labelled, which is good, but they are not decisions supplied by A9. A9's signature ratifies its own recorded OWNER items, not automatically every later owner addition to an unregistered profile. This is a provenance mismatch, not a demand for another author ruling.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:85:**Proposed fix:** describe the document as an extraction plus a separately listed set of dated profile decisions, and state the governing registration/adoption mechanism. Preserve the new decisions and corrections visibly; do not disguise them as A9 citations.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:| 1. Fixed path; exactly canonical TXT bytes, no LF | Sound profile choice. P8 §7.2 explicitly delegates path and framing; A9 requires the same value/window. Modes 100644/40000 are additional profile restrictions, not A9 requirements. | No demonstrated false accept in linkage/parser. LF or executable-file rejection is intentional conformance rejection once registered, not an inherent security necessity. Fixed-path enforcement and actual DNS equality are not yet exercised (F7). Present nonconforming evidence should be INVALID; missing objects UNVERIFIABLE. |
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:94:| 4. No snapshot proof => UNVERIFIABLE, no substitute time | Sound and consistent with A9 C4 and P8 §6. A supplied bad proof is a failed check, hence INVALID, rather than “missing anchor.” | No need for current DNS or a current clock in 2106. Do not promote a missing proof using receipt T or capture time. Real omission crashes today (F4); explicit diagnostic overrides must remain outside V1 acceptance. |
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:97:## Decisions stated in the profile that A9 does not settle
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:99:This inventory distinguishes new choices from inherited encoding/spec details. A9 binding the signing-commit ENC baseline is sufficient provenance for ENC decisions; they are not all new profile decisions.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:105:5. §5: repository key described specifically as the public half of an offline root. Its source is the operations runbook, not an A9 requirement. Keep it an operating fact or explicitly adopt it as profile policy.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:106:6. §2.1/§3: the twelve-member inventory and ten-stage presentation elaborate ENC/SPEC. A9 does not itself decide this exact container/member list or a stage order. The profile correctly leaves container encoding and stage reason precedence OPEN; the table must not be mistaken for a newly fixed execution order.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:109:The substantial OPEN list is otherwise candid: container, dispatch fields, binding forms, M5, git timing, pin format/coverage/conflicts, snapshot body and time derivation remain unresolved. The profile does not falsely say the hybrid branch or its id is chosen. The signed ENC baseline supplies spec id, fingerprint schemes, canonical grammar, signature serialization and number details; those do not contradict A9 merely because A9 does not repeat them.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:113:A verifier running in 2106 must evaluate the archived evidence at the proof-derived historical times under the same governing specification and trust configuration, not at 2106 or using then-current root publication status. The current default DNSSEC evaluation follows that principle. Past revocation/removal does not retroactively remove a pinned historical root. The same inputs should reproduce the same verdict, including existing false rejects; reproducibility is not evidence that the cutoff policy was sound. Historical compromise/algorithm safety remains a separate assessment under A9.4. No renewal or proof of security through 2106 is being established here. Also keep epoch arithmetic wider than 32 bits; archived DNS RRSIG fields must be interpreted under their historical time semantics, not as current-date freshness checks.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:118:- `cd prototype && uv run python spike/selftest.py`: **26/26 controls passed**, exit 0. Its KSK-2024 rollover calls use explicitly stated fixture time because that capture has no OTS proof; this is not an anchored end-to-end positive vector.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:120:- Passing tests do not cover the counterexamples above. The original spike still lacks DNS key publication and git-channel evidence, uses an older receipt layout, and excludes DNSKEY_PUBLICATION from its CLI required groups. Its exit 0 is therefore not a V1-conformance or C4 result; SPIKE-REPORT.md already records these limits.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md:122:Recommendation: retain the fixed-path/exact-bytes and pinned-domain choices, repair the existential and typed-evidence handling, and settle the retirement-validation and snapshot-to-receipt time policies explicitly before the profile freeze. No claim is discharged by this review.
docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-BRIEF.md:8:Write findings with severity and proposed fix to docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:69:| G02 / git authority time | Must the git assertion predate the receipt, its declaration, or its T? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:345-347`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:630-634`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:326-328` | **Depends on OPEN / ambiguous.** E says authority evidence is fixed “before any receipt”; P expressly leaves the relation undecided. A9 fixes the repository-key interval check at the commit's own time, not its relation to receipt T. | Stop. A signed assertion anchored after receipt T but during the repository key's signing interval could pass an evidence-time-only reading and fail a before-T reading. Do not infer public publication from git evidence. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:73:| G06 / snapshot time | How does the snapshot's own OTS proof select its anchored time, and at what depth? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:396-412`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:536-542`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:307-312` | **Depends on OPEN.** The own-anchor requirement is fixed; its derivation and confirmation depth are not. | Stop. A multi-attestation snapshot with one time inside and another outside an RRSIG window, or with fewer than six confirmations, separates choices. Never use capture time, receipt T or current wall time as a substitute. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:78:| G11 / signature validation policy | Beyond mathematical ECDSA, must hashed critical/unknown subpackets, creation time, expiry or issuer metadata affect acceptance? Does the manifest possession proof use the same serialization rule? | `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:324-337`, `:366-370`, `:444-468`, `:498-513`, `:654-658`, `:806-807`; `formal/p8/PROOF.md:105-110` | **Silent at verifier-policy layer.** Codec explicitly ignores subpacket contents; unhashed area has no meaning and key expiry is not relied on. P3 profile is outside P8. Hashed critical-content handling and possession serialization are not settled here. | Stop at math/policy verification; do not import a generic OpenPGP keyring policy. A mathematically verifying packet with an unknown critical hashed subpacket can pass pure ECDSA verification and fail a standards-oriented OpenPGP validator. Do not add revocation or current-time expiry checks. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:79:| G12 / algorithm policy | What governing specification hash, frozen signer branch and full allowed/prohibited identifier policy is installed? | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:146-148`, `:268-278`, `:386-391`, `:523`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:83-85`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:438-442`, `:480-489` | **Depends on OPEN / silent.** Classical id is concrete; branch/id/supplement and original prohibited-id cases are not furnished. | Parse classical O and unknown opaque slots only; no PQ registration assumed. A classical receipt can pass a fallback profile and fail a hybrid-required profile; an eventual pair id cannot be guessed from ML-DSA-65's name. Governing policy must be installed independently, not supplied by receipt content. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:83:| G16 / DNSSEC algorithm and validation profile | Which exact DNSSEC algorithms/editions, canonical signed-data rules, delegation handling and unsupported-algorithm outcomes govern? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:290-295`, `:398-407`, `:436`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:141`, `:158`, `:307-323` | **Silent here / external dependency.** Root existential rule and selected witness timing are concrete, but the full DNSSEC verifier profile and normative dependency closure are absent. | Stop; no network/RFC lookup or library used to fill policy. An algorithm in the historical chain supported by one validator and unsupported by another changes performability; an extra invalid RRSIG must not defeat a valid witness (P:403-406 resolves that part). | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:89:| G17 / container and dispatch | What file/container format, names, binary encoding, duplicate-member policy, layer representation and request field does dispatch read? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:113-116`, `:126-135`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:741-748`, `:802`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:285` | **Depends on OPEN.** Unsigned-label dispatch is decided but locating it and recognizing wrappers/requests is not. | Stop at full-bundle entry. CLI raw-file arguments are explicitly probe-only. The same uninterpretable input could yield OUT_OF_PROFILE, an input error, or an in-profile INVALID under different containers. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:94:| G22 / root trust input | What are the exact KSK bytes and complete starts/ends/publication/revocation/L/source fields, and how are they obtained authentically? | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:152-155`, `:307-323`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:215-251` | **Silent for material/format; partial dates supplied.** Naming KSK-2017/2024 is not a cryptographic trust anchor. Starts and open-end representation are incomplete. | Stop; do not retrieve keys or guess absent dates. Use existential acceptance only after trusted key material, reliance intervals and cryptographic witnesses exist. Wrong or missing keys change authentication/performability. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:97:| G25 / git parser and signature profile | How are duplicate headers, folded gpgsig, multiple signatures, armored payloads, duplicate tree entries and noncanonical tree objects handled? Which repository signing algorithms are supported? | `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:692-711`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:314-341`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:295-305`, `:326-328` | **Silent here / external dependency.** The raw hash formula, path/modes and signature-removal purpose are supplied; complete git object/signature grammar and repo key scheme are not. | Stop; no subprocess git/gpg or existing implementation consulted. First-versus-last duplicate path/header selection can change authenticated blob interpretation. A robust implementation needs a declared grammar/duplicate policy. A9 settles sha1-only descriptor and exact stamped hex+LF; those are not open gaps. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:99:| G27 / historical DNS time representation | How are 32-bit DNSSEC times interpreted at rollover, and is evaluation always reconstructed historical snapshot time rather than the 2106 wall clock? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:398-407`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:311-315`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:436-450` | **Silent here / external dependency.** Historical evaluation time is specified; DNSSEC serial-time arithmetic and epoch conventions are not reproduced or pinned to a named normative section. | Stop at DNSSEC. Historical 2026 evidence must be evaluated at its verified snapshot time, not “now.” Archive the requisite DNSSEC time rules; ordinary integer versus serial-time interpretation can change a boundary verdict. This is a dependency-closure need, not a demand for future issuance. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:114:| G37 / adoption and source baseline | Which source state governs this experiment given draft labels and references to signing/freeze hashes? | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:3-14`, `:24-26`, `:146-148`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:3-7`, `:43-47` | **Silent distribution baseline, resolved task authority.** Four live documents are supplied, not their signing commit or supplement. | Treat A9 as governing per user instruction; owner profile decisions as proposed design for the probe; no adoption claim. Real frozen hash/baseline acceptance is G12/G13, not silently assumed here. | **no** |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md:116:| G39 / stale git/single-window profile text | Does V1 reject successors or omit repository signing intervals/object_format because P does not carry latest A9 edits? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:299-307`, `:329-331`, `:314-320`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:295-305`, `:324-332`, `:398-412` | **Contradictory/stale extraction, resolved by A9.** A9 fixes sha1 descriptor outcomes, 40 lowercase hex+LF, repository signing intervals, possible same-path registered replacement, and per-receipt window checks rather than cross-receipt overlap. | Follow A9. Do not check rotation authorization or pairwise non-overlap in verifier. Do not accept SHA-256 git objects. Commit and snapshot proof-time derivations still stop under G02–G06. | **no** |
