docs/reviews/2026-07-07-codex-amendment-2-review.md:46:   (`formal/tla/P5c_IssuanceProtocol.tla`, lines 113–122) under a single-clock
docs/reviews/2026-07-07-codex-amendment-2-review.md:73:- **Medium:** The “one predicate, both sides” claim is stronger than the issuer model supports. A2.1 defines `confirmed_at` as a chain-visible block timestamp in `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:66`, while P5c `Ship` still checks `now <= declared + Delta` in `formal/tla/P5c_IssuanceProtocol.tla:117`. The header admits this works only under the single-clock abstraction in line 23. That may be acceptable, but then the draft/model should say the correspondence is by abstraction, not “exact by construction.”
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/A9-GAP-FIXES-CONFIRMATION.md:9:| F01 | **APPLIED** | A9.1 claim, lines 131–134, and C4, line 143, require chain-order pre-commitment: the selected git height plus five must be strictly below the selected receipt height. C4 explicitly disclaims UTC pre-commitment and DNSSEC publication-before-issue. The change is visibly labelled OWNER DECISION / BROADEN. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/A9-GAP-FIXES-CONFIRMATION.md:26:Presentation of an earlier qualifying git witness can only decrease the selected g and cannot defeat this honest ordering. A genuinely earlier receipt witness for those exact newly frozen signatures would contradict the assumptions used above. Header-clock skew does not invalidate the height argument. Other timestamp, window, signature and coverage checks still apply; this confirms that the new inequality adds no false rejection to the wait-first path, not that waiting guarantees every other check will pass.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/A9-GAP-FIXES-CONFIRMATION.md:32:The new exclusion is consistent with the adversary paragraph: authority-channel capture remains in scope, while replacement of the independently authenticated verifier distribution is an explicit external boundary. The prohibition on AWS access to the release key also avoids conflating the slot-2 signing queue's capabilities in A9.6 with release authority. Append-only configuration obligations in A9.5 and the identical-declared-input stability contract in A9.4 remain applicable to authorized releases; the new authentication rule does not replace them. A9.1's signed, versioned trust set is compatible with independent release allowlisting and is not permission to accept any configuration signed by a channel key.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/A9-GAP-FIXES-CONFIRMATION.md:36:The disposition leaves release-key custody OPEN. A9 states the required authorization boundary; this pass does not establish that a dated custody record or deployed boundary exists. The captured-repository-signer negative test, selection/boundary vectors and release/bootstrap details remain implementation and freeze obligations, not evidence supplied by this diff.
docs/reviews/2026-09-15-codex-full-review-ledger-round4.md:16:3. **C-Q1 drops accepted-evidence binding in degraded mode.** ACCEPTED. `LayerAccepted` carries the evidence term (or a reference) and C-Q1 requires evidence-to-this-acceptance binding in both modes; only the honest-publication conclusion is dropped in degraded-compromised mode, per A3 §A3.2's fewer-but-never-zero evidence.
docs/reviews/2026-09-15-codex-full-review-ledger-round4.md:94:**Repair:** retain an evidence-to-this-acceptance binding requirement in both modes. Drop only the honest-publication conclusion in degraded-compromised mode. A declared but unconsumed `AuthorityEvidence` event does not close this link.
docs/reviews/2026-09-17-codex-review-capstone-predictions-round11.md:101:The requested repairs for the thirty-three accepted findings are present; finding 1 extends the retained-list audit rather than reversing an earlier disposition. The three ledger corrections remain operative: structural exact-byte checking, publication provenance as a correspondence, and fresh intake identifiers with layer-indexed completion. (`formal/suite/capstone/PREDICTIONS.md:115–162`, `:350–365`, `:393–438`, `:1280–1300`.)
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:5:Authority for this review is Amendment 9, as instructed, despite its PROPOSED status. The owner still decides the resolutions before 30 Oct; this review decides none of them. A9 outranks the profile and the draft. Read in full: RESOLUTIONS-DRAFT, GAPS, A9, V1-PROFILE, ENCODING-V1. Prototype reads were used to check artifact claims, never to supply missing normative rules. Existing workspace changes were left alone. Only this review file was written.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:13:**Location:** draft §D/G02, lines 438–458; §B/G03; E §7.1; A9 C4/C5 and A9.6 chain-time residual.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:17:**Distinguishing input:** an honestly signed receipt declares 12:00, anchors at height h with nTime 11:55, and obtains its five successors in time. Its declaration is within the signer's ε check. A subsequently manufactured signed assertion anchors at height j > h with nTime 11:59, during the configured repository-key interval. A DNS snapshot anchors at 12:10 with valid witness windows. Use the same genuine receipt keys, matching channel values, and a window containing declared and T. Every proposed temporal comparison passes, although the git assertion was made after the receipt existed and after its inclusion block. A9's channel-capture adversary can manufacture the authority evidence after capturing the relevant channel credentials, while retaining the uncompromised service's original signatures. This is a counterexample to the proposed pre-commitment explanation, not a forgery of those signatures or a demonstration of key reattribution. Capture of both authority channels is the premise of this version of the construction.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:19:The DNS correction is appropriate: DNSSEC at T_snap does not establish publication before issue. The replacement must not transfer that unsupported chronology claim to git. Waiting for git k=6 before honest issuance helps operationally but is not enforced by the proposed comparison and does not prove UTC ordering to a verifier. Equality also does not mean “before.”
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:27:**Label:** split CORRECT (withdraw chronology overclaim), new owner profile restriction (the comparison), and issuer practice (wait for k=6). Do not label the whole package CLARIFY. No honest shipped V1 counterexample to the wait-first revised issue path was found, but “ships complete” alone does not imply wait-before-sign.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:49:Lowest height is deterministic once qualification is fixed. It is not equivalent to lowest timestamp. Let two qualifying attestations have h1 < h2, `nTime(h1) = not-after + 1`, and `nTime(h2) = not-after`. Both depth segments and all other comparisons can pass. G03 gives ISSUANCE_WINDOW; smallest-nTime gives VALID_STRICT. Reverse the boundary relative to `declared - epsilon` and the acceptance direction reverses. Nonmonotone nTime is allowed without a deep reorganization or hash break.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:55:> Earliest means lowest Bitcoin block height among qualifying attestations, not least nTime. Both T and confirmed_at come from that selected segment. Nonmonotone timestamps can make another selection rule return a different verdict on the same proof. This profile fixes the chain-order reading of A9 C5.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:57:Have the owner confirm that reading against the registered C5 sentence; amend if chronological minimum was intended. Add nonmonotone two-branch tests at c1, c2 and not-after, and one test with both branches at the same height. No signed layout or existing P8 vector must change for this reading. The draft's “up to about 2 h” should be removed as a bound on nonmonotonicity: the forward-clock check does not establish that general bound.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:63:The key finding is correct under its stated deployment condition. If a captured repository credential is also an accepted configuration-update signer, an attacker signs a config adding their own root KSK, repository key and header pins. They create service-looking frames and signatures under their own keys, a matching manifest, their own DNSSEC chain and git assertion, and fabricated headers with chosen merkle roots/times. The authentic verifier then accepts fabricated presentation evidence without a service issuance event. Pins no longer represent authentic mainnet. This does not require breaking SHA-256: it compromises the authority deciding which hash is trusted.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:67:A distinct key can still be callable using the captured channel account, repository credentials or shared signing service. Key inequality alone does not establish independence. Calling all capture of that key “outside A8.3” without specifying the access boundary introduces an assumption by declaration.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:73:Choose either the dedicated-key update model or independent release allowlisting, and specify bootstrap, key rotation, version/genesis values and invalid-config no-verdict behavior. Keep “distribution compromise” as an explicit external assumption, and test a captured repository signer trying to authorize a config. Generating a third token is insufficient evidence by itself.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:75:**Label/amendment:** new trust-distribution design plus explicit assumption, not mere CLARIFY. With genuinely independent authorization this repairs an underspecified trust input and needs no protection concession. If independence cannot be supplied and a channel credential can rewrite distributed trust, then a NARROW-MODEL/amendment excluding that capture path is required. “No NARROW-MODEL needed” is conditional on implementing the boundary, not established by selecting a distinct public key. Config bytes acquire a new signature, although receipt-frame layouts need not change.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:81:The algorithm allowlist is a legitimate visible NARROW-CLAIM, not a demonstrated NARROW-MODEL. Unsupported algorithms must never become an insecure-but-accepted chain. However, the table “per required RRset” says a supported signature validating at T_snap passes and does not include KSK reliance eligibility. G22 supplies byte equality, not the missing period check. Another implementer will import A9's period rule; a literal implementation of the generalized table can pass a root signed by a trusted retired KSK outside reliance. A captured retired KSK can produce such a signature without breaking its algorithm. A9 governs and forbids this acceptance, so the final text must expressly preserve it.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:115:**Exact proposed fix:** adopt a small explicit policy: exactly one hashed type-2 subpacket with four data octets; recognize it whether critical or not; reject unknown critical hashed subpackets; state the treatment of each other recognized critical type. Either ignore all unhashed semantics consistently with E §3.5, or visibly choose a new transport/policy restriction and say why. Specify whether type 3 is forbidden in hashed area only and retain the “never wall-clock expiry” rule. If the blanket ban is retained, label it NARROW-CLAIM/new owner signature profile and constrain the issuer adapter before signing/stamping.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:117:Existing SIG-01/02 need not change. Add signature-profile acceptance/rejection vectors distinct from P8 codec vectors; do not change codec expectations merely to impose this policy. More generally “no signed byte changes” must distinguish a new issuer's allowed signature output from an unchanged frame layout.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:129:Define group boundaries using owner, type and covered type, signer-zone/ancestor constraints, root-signer case and the empty/missing sequence distinction. Keep explicit no-wildcard scope as NARROW-CLAIM. Conversion from resolver messages changes snapshot body S and SHA256(S), so previously stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused. It requires a fresh snapshot stamp, although P8's envelope construction and existing vectors need not change. No documented conforming explicit-owner V1 receipt is shown falsely rejected after fresh capture/conversion; the archived spike is not already a conforming V1 bundle.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:143:**Location:** draft G21 lines 167–178; A9.6; E §5.2.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:155:Decimal strings have no canonical grammar/range: headers can contain both `"1"` and `"01"`, creating a normalization/duplicate-height fork. Repository entries are `[…]`, not a fully specified schema; key-to-D pairing, time field types and interval constraints must be concrete. Genesis previous_sha256, version ordering, profile value, root provenance/publication/revocation types, and dependency-manifest top-level structure remain unspecified. A matching frozen hash can make dependency bytes unique at runtime, but it does not tell the freeze author what object to construct.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:175:**Exact proposed fix:** require an archived authenticated source containing each full public key, or obtain it from an archived root DNSKEY RRset and cross-check the DS digest against independently authenticated IANA material. Record exact RDATA and complete interval starts/ends/L in the actual pre-freeze config, with source digests and the trusted ICANN CA/bootstrap procedure for .p7s verification. Keep publication/revocation informational as A9 requires. Replace “after 2027-01-11” with “when the presented RDATA has REVOKE set”; the period gate supplies temporal exclusion. No additional receipt bytes or P8 vectors need change.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:184:| G02 | Numeric rule unique; chronology meaning remains wrong. | F01: post-receipt assertion can satisfy purported pre-commitment. | Wait-first future issue path can pass; prior complete-at-ship alone does not guarantee it. | CORRECT plus new profile restriction; author/amendment conditional on C4 chronology meaning. F01. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:188:| G06 | Same depth/T rule is precise; c3 is newly added. | None found; adds a check. | Snapshot stamped before receipt need not confirm earlier; distinct calendars/branches can confirm in different order. Receipt c3 passing does not imply snapshot c3. | Label snapshot c3 a new owner acceptance restriction, not existing CLARIFY. Require issuer check and recapture/retry before ship; add receipt-pass/snapshot-c3-fail control. No P8 layout change. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:190:| G08 | Bootstrap, complete schema and authorization boundary incomplete. | F04's conditional channel-credential/config rewrite attack. | Conforming independently authenticated config passes; failure is no-verdict input error. | New distribution design/assumption. NARROW-MODEL required only if channel capture can authorize configs and is excluded. F04/F11. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:193:| G11 | Top table/prose expiration mismatch; type-2 payload rule missing. | Malformed type-2 metadata can pass a type-count-only reading; no service forgery shown. | Standards-conforming critical creation time rejected; current vectors pass. | New restrictive signature profile; no demonstrated NARROW-MODEL. F07. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:199:| G17 | Dispatch clear in principle; missing/schema exception and numeric-string grammar incomplete. | No unsafe in-profile accept identified. OUT_OF_PROFILE is rejection. | Reindentation invalidates unsigned transport; intentional new custody restriction. | New container/canonicality policy, not inherited rule. F11. No claim that unsigned transport redesign requires a P8 tag. |
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:225:Do not approve §K's blanket “none” as written. The file's draft status is not an exemption from A9: A9 incorporates an ENCODING-V1 signing baseline and registers C4/C5/C6 meanings and the object_format outcomes. A later unregistered profile cannot override those by relabeling a change as wording.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:229:There is no established unavoidable NARROW-MODEL finding. There are two conditional author surfaces: excluding a channel-capture path because config authorization is shared (F04), and weakening an actual pre-issue commitment claim to a numeric chain-time comparison (F01). Implement the missing authorization boundary or correct the claim explicitly; do not announce that either issue has already disappeared. G05 requires a registered reading, not an invented cryptographic security claim about all-branch coverage.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:231:“No signed byte changes” is only defensible as **no mandatory attestation-frame layout change for these resolutions**. Freezing edited spec/vector objects changes their signed hashes; converting the DNS body changes its anchored S preimage and needs a fresh stamp; trust configuration gets a new detached signature; signature policies can change allowed signed signature output. Existing archived proof bytes cannot be converted in place while retaining their stamp. The authorized hybrid supplement still changes slot encoding and requires its own vectors. Existing fallback P8 golden/rejection expectations need not be rewritten merely to add above-codec policies, but boundary and full-profile vectors remain necessary.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:240:- Primary standards checked online: [RFC 9580](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9580.html), [RFC 4034](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4034.html), [RFC 4035](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4035.html), [RFC 1982](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1982.html), and [RFC 9718](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9718.html). RFC 1982 leaves the half-range ordering undefined; the profile must choose an outcome. The live IANA XML tool fetch failed, so no claim is made about today's file containing particular optional public-key elements.
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/CODEX-REVIEW-RESOLUTIONS.md:241:- No formal model was run, no real full-profile forged bundle was issued, and no C-row, preregistered gate or implementation readiness was discharged. Counterexamples above distinguish semantic rules and assumptions from successful attacks on an implemented V1 verifier.
