docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:1:# Adversarial review: UTC bound and capture visibility
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:9:Read the complete decision memo, complete A9 and proposed A10; A2 §A2.1; A8 §A8.3/§A8.5; V1-PROFILE §5/§7/§15.5, and the output, authority, catalog, open-item and precedence provisions those sections invoke. A9 in the working tree is byte-identical to its version at signing commit `2ad34890359c446efaebcb116388b8e9ffbf6428`; the reviewed repository HEAD was `7d7f94d797048f96df9a2b129962a5604439f4ae`. A10 and the memo were untracked, proposed material. Memo locators below refer to `docs/decisions/2026-10-17-utc-bound-and-capture-visibility.md` before any revisions.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:21:**Counterexample:** use seconds relative to an arbitrary epoch. The bytes exist at 99, and both TSAs issue at 100. Token A says `genTime=0, accuracy=100`; token B says `genTime=90, accuracy=10`. Both clock intervals include issuance. `max(genTime)=90` is earlier than the bytes, while `max(genTime+accuracy)=100` is a valid upper endpoint.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:> For every required token i, determine a finite authenticated accuracy allowance a_i, from the token or its pinned policy. Report U_TSA = max_i(genTime_i + a_i), rounded upward if the report has coarser precision. Under the commitment assumptions, this bounds existence if at least one required TSA's clock and signing authority were trustworthy for its token. A token with no established finite allowance cannot supply this finite bound. Taking the minimum requires stronger trust assumptions.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:33:The risks are real, but the proposed remedy and comparison are too categorical. Anchoring a token preserves its exact bytes against fabrication *after that anchor*. It does not establish that the TSA's clock or authority was sound when it produced the token. A thief can manufacture a falsely backdated token and then anchor it. Establishing historical trust needs a trustworthy cutoff and evidence that the preserved token predates it; a header's nominal UTC time alone cannot establish that comparison. This is the same distinction A9.4/A9.6 already make for service signatures.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:43:> U2 adds TSA clock, certification and key-use trust. For the long-horizon preservation candidate, retaining independent evidence of the token before later compromise is additional work. U1 instead adds the unverified A-UTC assumption to the existing commitment/history assumptions.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:51:**Location:** memo :208, :222–234; A9 :162, :267–280; profile :288–329.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:53:A9.4 guarantees the six-field result under the same governing specification and other declared inputs. The memo recognizes that “six” would become “seven”, but its minimal change list does not complete the output-format and compatibility consequences. The profile specifies the stable JSON object, diagnostic wrapper, CLI shape, dispatch-null behavior and multi-failure agreement cases. Merely computing another value in both implementations does not settle those interfaces.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:55:**Does this break A9.4?** Inserting a mandatory seventh field into the old stable object changes the registered contract. It cannot be presented as unchanged conformance to that same governing specification. An explicit A10 supersession before the profile freeze can legitimately define the revised contract under the resulting specification hash. It does not retroactively authorize changing results for an older specification. Adding a value outside the stable six is a smaller alternative.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:63:If the owner deliberately wants a seventh **stable** field instead, explicitly supersede A9.1 :162 and every relevant A9.4 six-field clause, and update profile §3.4, §3.5, the scheduling/completeness rule, agreement cases and report consumers. Bind this behavior to the amended frozen specification; preserve old-spec behavior if any older-spec bundles are ever supported. This is bounded work, but it belongs in the cost and amendment inventory. The current pre-freeze state makes the change possible; it does not make it a wording-only change.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:### F04 — MEDIUM: the profile does not supply Bitcoin-era unwrapping, and U must not use uint32 arithmetic
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:67:**Location:** memo :74–75, :230–234; profile §7.1 :803–805 and §7.3 :868–873.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:69:The profile interprets Bitcoin `nTime` as its raw unsigned 32-bit epoch-seconds value. Its serial-time unwrapping rule applies to **DNSSEC RRSIG** fields relative to T_snap. It does not infer an era for Bitcoin timestamps. The memo's statement that Bitcoin's wrap is already handled is therefore unsupported.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:75:> Verification of a preserved V1 receipt does not consult the current clock and can still run in 2106. Interpret each bundled nTime as the profile's unsigned epoch-seconds value, widen it before arithmetic, and compute U without modular reduction. This does not define timestamps for newly mined post-wrap Bitcoin blocks; that would require a future Bitcoin/profile rule.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:81:Add the maximum-uint32 boundary vector and a preserved-old-receipt verification case with a 2106 verifier clock. No new Bitcoin-era design is needed for V1's old receipts.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:85:**Location:** memo :162–168 and :198–201; A9.5 :326–332; profile §14.2 item 1.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:87:The failure-mode paragraph correctly names the existing NARROW-MODEL route. The final recommendation then appears to allow “cut the listing” and return to A10.4 option (a) if release independence is unavailable. That is incomplete: independent release authentication already protects V1's KSKs, repository trust and header pins against this adversary. Its failure is not specific to the new key list. Merely deleting `service_keys` cannot restore the original capture claim.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:> If the listing is cut for schedule reasons while A9.5's independent release boundary is established, retain A10.4 option (a) and state the absence of new-key protection. If release independence itself cannot be established, follow profile §14.2 item 1: present a NARROW-MODEL of the affected channel-capture claim to the author before freeze. Cutting the listing alone does not resolve that existing prerequisite.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:95:### F06 — LOW: distinguish node clock from UTC, and a BIP implementation proposal from activation
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:99:The memo correctly identifies the **receiving validating node's system clock**, rather than the miner's clock or current peer-adjusted time. Bitcoin Core 27.0 removed network-adjusted time from this code. The subsequent inference “above real time” still needs that node's clock-error assumption. [Core 27.0 release notes](https://bitcoincore.org/en/releases/27.0/), [header validation](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/v27.0/src/validation.cpp)
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:103:> This bounds nTime above the receiving node's clock at acceptance. If that clock is accurate to UTC, it implies acceptance no earlier than nTime − 2 h in UTC; otherwise its clock-error bound must also be included. It supplies no real-time upper bound.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:123:> The initial release independently authorizes and records the complete service-key schedule through as_of. Later releases retain every existing entry unchanged, introduce no overlapping interval, and add an entry only if its not_before is strictly greater than the previous as_of. Thus absence through a past horizon is permanently closed. Completeness concerns authorized entries, not arbitrary channel assertions. The release authority must not authorize a new service-key tuple solely because the two potentially captured channels assert it.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:133:**Counts and clocks:** h* is the receipt's **inclusion** block. A2.1 counts that block as confirmation one, so confirmation six is h*+5. T is nTime(h*); `confirmed_at` is nTime(h*+5). U uses the maximum of **all six**, not just either endpoint, and not the minimum. The DNS and git proofs' selected heights do not enter this receipt U. The selected receipt branch remains the lowest qualifying height, not the branch with the most favorable timestamp. Qualification already requires the six linked, pinned headers. There is no confirmation-count off-by-one.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:146:**How minimal is A-UTC?** It needs only one one-sided lag witness, not an honest inclusion miner, an honest majority of the six, synchronized clocks for every miner, or a bound on service observation delay. That is a small, intelligible operational assumption. It is not the logically weakest sufficient assumption: `max nTime + 7200 ≥ r(h*)` would itself suffice, and a successor timestamp could satisfy that without approximating its own mining time. Retain A-UTC because its per-block interpretation is intelligible; call it “a sufficient one-sided assumption”, not a uniquely minimal one. Neither m=6 nor Δ=7200 is established as optimal. Two hours is an author-selected backward tolerance; matching Bitcoin's forward tolerance does not validate it.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:163:This preserves positive witnesses and the classifier's closed-negative conclusions. It does **not** establish whole-verifier monotonicity across configurations: header-pin extensions can change qualifying selection, T, U and other checks (profile §5 :524–531). A9.4 requires identical stable output for identical declared inputs, including the same governing specification and configuration. Do not replace that with an unconditional same-verdict promise across releases. The memo's local argument is compatible with that contract when read this way.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:173:**BROADEN is correct** for an additional conditional UTC assessment and for the narrower new-key non-acceptance guarantee. It must remain paired with the explicit A-UTC assumption and release-authority boundary. It is not evidence that the old unconditional UTC reading was sound, or that capture becomes publicly visible. Keep the existing NARROW-CLAIM corrections for the old prose and keep the original two-channel checks.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:175:The minimum amendment inventory also needs the service-key list in A9.1's trust-configuration description and A9.5's append-only entry list, not only C4/adversary/boundaries; a scoped statement in A9.6's first-window discussion; the new assessment and assumption in the output/catalog specification; and the precise cut/fallback rule. Freeze Δ=7200 and m=6 in the governing specification, not mutable trust-file policy. Preserve guarantee 2's chain-time meaning. Do not turn the nearby removal of “V1 does not establish” into an unqualified UTC guarantee for that window rule.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/CODEX-REVIEW.md:181:The proof above is the principal arithmetic argument. A read-only finite check exercised 343,937 ordered-mining/arbitrary-header assignments satisfying the lag assumption in a small integer domain, with no inequality violation. A separate 14,280-case finite check of allowed single service-list additions preserved prior pass and INVALID results. These checks illustrate the arguments; they are not Bitcoin-conformance tests or formal discharge. The uint32 boundary and the TSA accuracy counterexample were evaluated directly. No real captured-channel receipt vector was executed in this review.
