docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/DISPOSITION.md:4:`docs/decisions/2026-10-17-utc-bound-and-capture-visibility.md`, using Codex's exact text wherever the review gave
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/DISPOSITION.md:23:  for a 2106 verifier clock.
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/DISPOSITION.md:27:- **F06 (LOW), ACCEPTED.** The future rule is stated relative to the receiving node's clock, with its clock-error
docs/reviews/utc-capture-memo/DISPOSITION.md:41:  - the amendment inventory gains A9.1's trust item, A9.5's list and A9.6's first window;
docs/reviews/2026-09-13-codex-review-implementation-spec.md:125:**Record agrees:** The registered confirmation bound is inclusive, `confirmed_at ≤ declared_issue_time + δ`. This concerns the designated block timestamp, not wall-clock arrival. (`docs/implementation-spec.md:1259–1264,1284–1286`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:190–196,218–248`)
docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/DISPOSITION-RESOLUTIONS.md:6:- F04 CRITICAL (conditional): accepted. A9.5 now has "Trust-configuration authority": independent release allowlisting (each verifier release compiles in its config hash), signed by a release key that no authority-channel credential can use, with distribution compromise as a stated boundary. Custody of that key is OPEN, for the author (see the note to Tony): YubiKey 2 is not in hand. A test is required in which a captured repository signer tries to authorize a config.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:3:Reviewed 2026-10-16. **Result: revision required before reliance or publication.**
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:45:| S11 | 16 | “The content and both signatures existed at the anchor time T or before T.” | 14 | **FAITHFUL** | A9 36–37; source clock ambiguity A01 remains. |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:90:| S56 | 72 | “*Not claimed:* that the git record was public.” | 6 | **FAITHFUL** | C4; no proof of public publication. The issuer’s publication duty remains (A9 495–500). |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:| S57 | 72 | “*Not claimed:* an order in clock time.” | 5 | **FAITHFUL** | C4; no UTC-order claim. |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:93:| S59 | 75 | “The signed parts and their exact signatures existed at time T or before T.” | 14 | **FAITHFUL** | C5; exact signatures included; source clock ambiguity A01 remains. |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:110:| S76 | 94 | “Examples: the "existential" wording in C2; the pinned-header rule in C6; the missing-capture negative control in C7.” | 17 | **FAITHFUL** | Editorial examples. “At some time” preserves the existential quantifier; F11 concerns the issuance event. |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:133:**S10 and S51 — DROPS the evidence-time qualification.** Pilot: “Two authority records said that these keys belong to wamason.com, for a stated window.” / “Two authority records said that the keys belong to wamason.com, for the window.” C4: “authenticated K for D, with the issuance window, at their evidence times.” These are historical assertions evaluated at particular evidence times, not a claim of continuing authority. The later descriptions never restore those evaluation times.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:197:**S34 — CHANGES whole operational capture into simultaneous account capture.** Pilot: “An attacker who controls all of the author's accounts at the same time.” A9 49: “whole-author-sphere capture (R-2.15)”. The referenced R-2.15 says “the author's entire operational sphere at issue time”; A1 452–458 makes the same distinction. The sphere includes operational control beyond accounts, and the source names issue time, not any simultaneous compromise at any time.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:202:V1 does not protect against capture of the author's whole operational sphere at issue time.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:319:There is **no arithmetic off-by-one error** in the pilot's “at least six blocks before” by itself: integer heights satisfying `g + 5 < r` have `r − g ≥ 6`. The sixth confirmation of the git anchor is at `g + 5`; the receipt anchor must follow it. This is distinct from source issue A03 about reorganization depth.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:373:A01 addresses the source's own “existed no later than T” wording. Do not repair that source ambiguity by inventing a numeric clock-error bound.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:436:**S22 — DROPS disclosure of authority credentials that does not amount to account or repository control.** Pilot: “The attacker controls the DNS account or the git repository, but cannot sign with the service keys.” A9 41–42: “captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays uncompromised”. The trust-boundary explanation names “the DNS credential” and “the repository key” separately (A9 327–331). Possession of the repository signing key is not the same thing as control of the hosted repository, particularly when C4 does not prove public publication. The plain wording must include that credential exposure, rather than replace it with host or account control.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:445:Define authority-record credentials and the repository key. A04 separately asks which receipt properties A9 protects after that capture; preserving the source's credential scope does not resolve that question.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:482:Pilot C4 non-claims: “Not claimed: revocation checks. Not claimed: that the git record was public. Not claimed: an order in clock time.” C4 also says: “The verifier fixes the required set”; “Neither channel commits to any receipt”; and “DNSSEC evidence establishes no publication before issue.” All three are missing. Historical authority evidence is not authority endorsement of this individual receipt. DNS evidence does not inherit the git evidence's pre-commitment order.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:493:C4's git public-publication non-claim remains. A9 495–500 separately retains public publication as an issuer duty that the verifier cannot prove.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:511:Pilot C7 mentions only: “The relying-party test on 4 December 2026 checks this. That test has not occurred yet.” C7 also says: “Assigned build evidence includes A9.4's missing-capture negative control.” A9 282–283 specifies: “for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.” That is separate build evidence, not the human comprehension test. No pilot sentence states it.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:519:- Only status capture is missing.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:521:- It gives INVALID only because status capture is missing.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:522:- It gives CANNOT VERIFY only because status capture is missing.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:525:Define status capture as the historical status evidence that V1 does not require, and define companion verifier and negative control. This does not allow a missing signature, required authority record or pinned header to pass.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:531:### A01 — “existed no later than T” needs an explicit clock meaning
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:558:If a reorganization's depth means the number of old tip blocks removed, replacing six blocks at that point can remove the anchor. “Deeper than six” excludes seven and above, leaving the equality case apparently covered. If the source uses another depth convention, it must state it. The model's `DepthK` guard is not itself a definition of the ordinary phrase “history deeper than k”.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:560:**Next amendment finding:** define reorganization depth and state the exact excluded equality boundary relative to confirmation count. Check the intended assumption against the model convention. This is a counting ambiguity with a concrete boundary case, not a claim that a model run failed. It is distinct from the correctly translated C4 six-block gap.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:562:### A04 — authority capture needs a stated property scope
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:564:A9 40–43 promises that verdicts hold against anyone who “captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)”. A8.3 72–76 defines credential disclosure with honest signing keys secret, but bounds its tested property to preventing reattribution of honest frames, alteration of honest signer sets, and carrying honest signatures onto other bytes or keys. A8.5 123–124 explicitly puts “preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new attestations” out of scope.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:566:A9's opening can read as protecting the *presentation-to-the-service claim for arbitrary new receipts* under credential capture. That is stronger than protecting an already signed receipt against reattribution. “Signing capability” also needs an object: capability for the original service keys, or for any keys a captured authority can name? Whole-author-sphere capture is excluded, but compromising authority credentials does not necessarily compromise the author's entire operational sphere.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:568:**Next amendment finding:** state whether the capture claim concerns existing honest receipts, new receipts, or both, and which credentials and signing keys it quantifies over. If new receipts are included, name the invariant that prevents attacker-selected key assertions from establishing service presentation. This is a scope ambiguity, not a demonstrated forged-receipt result. The pilot's short account-control bullet hides it rather than resolving it.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:589:- **Public publication:** C4's non-claim and A9 495–500's public-publication duty are compatible: the issuer must publish, but the verifier does not prove publication. The pilot must not remove that distinction.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:612:| 59; 62; 66; 72–73; 77; 81 | Repeated “Not claimed:” fragments | Reduced passive wording with no actor. Use “V1 does not establish …” or “V1 does not promise …”, with the actual non-claim as the object. For public publication: “The verifier does not establish that the git commit was public.” [D] |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:634:| 94–96 | The editorial notes use existential, pinned-header, missing-capture negative control, replay and reattribute terminology without dictionary entries. Keeping them internal does not make the entire pilot dictionary-only. |
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:645:- Add **signing capability**, **non-exportable**, **operational sphere**, **issuance event**, **signature algorithm**, **cryptographic assumption**, **key-use assumption**, **history assumption**, **revocation**, **historical security**, and **status capture**. Use the exact source meanings; possession of key bytes and permission to sign must remain distinct.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:649:- Add **standing**, **renewal**, **wrapper** and **degraded verification** before restoring O01. Define standing from the governing standing rules, not as mere key safety or revocation. Likewise, renewal is preservation with later evidence; a wrapper is an outer verification layer; degraded verification waives specified checks. These require their registered definitions before publication.
docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md:659:- **Bitcoin**, first at 16: explain that it supplies the chain evidence and the selected block's time. “Bitcoin fixes T” does not explain the system or its clock limitation.
docs/reviews/custody/DISPOSITION.md:11:- **C07 (MEDIUM) — ACCEPTED, applied.** §8 "Public-key bootstrap" adds an independent fingerprint recomputation, a check of each actual publication (freeze record, verifier and user documentation), retention outside the NUC, and an authenticated first-acquisition path (marked for the author).
docs/reviews/custody/DISPOSITION.md:12:- **C08 (MEDIUM) — ACCEPTED, applied.** The opening uses Codex's exact sentences; §11 adds the rehearsal record contents and Codex's eight-case table verbatim; the 15-minute figure is removed as unmeasured. The gate is now "before the first public verifier release" (author fact); pre-gate releases use the test release key; the format freezes 30 Oct. A10.4.2's wording ("before the freeze") is flagged for the author to amend.
