docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:5:The principal attack needs no stolen release key. A compromised NUC can prepare a malicious archive and a reassuring summary containing that archive's correct hash. The stated approval steps can then produce a genuine signature over an unwanted release. An offline key, a PIN and a touch do not establish which release the author intended to authorize.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:11:Read `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` in full, `docs/plain/STE-STYLE.md` in full, A10's release-authorization and custody provisions, A9.5's trust-configuration authority, V1-PROFILE §5 and §14.2 items 1–2, and V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK §6. The profile and runbook require authenticated bootstrap, rotation, bounded recovery and a defined signed release, in addition to storage separation. They still mark those implementation choices OPEN.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:The draft also omits the standing release checker. Checking whether additions are expected does not establish unchanged old entries, permitted endpoint closure, the hash/version chain or the proposed service-key authorization closure. A checker supplied by the captured NUC would not independently establish them either.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:86:**Locations:** :9–10, :58, :62–65. Governing requirements: profile:389–407 and :1154–1158; runbook:117–119.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:88:“Then the author signs it” can mean the archive, a digest string or the summary. “Signs ... the trust file” suggests a separate signature, while the governing profile expressly gives the config no signature: its hash is compiled into the signed verifier release. P-256/OpenPGP identifies a key scheme, not the complete release-verification format. There is no pinned verification command or check that the delivered archive contains the approved config and compiled hash.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:92:Require offline verification with the ceremony public key, and require a changed archive byte to fail. Verify the approved trust-file hash and frozen catalog binding in the actual distributed verifier; merely hashing files supplied beside a different executable is insufficient. Publish exactly the verified archive/signature pair. Replace :9–10 with: “The release key signs each verifier release. The verifier contains the hash of its trust file. The trust file has no separate signature.” If another format is selected, specify it equally precisely and review it before claiming this item closed.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:96:**Locations:** :72–75. Governing requirements: profile:1148–1158; runbook:134–145.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:98:Restoring a backup onto a new token leaves the old token able to use the same private key if its access controls can be satisfied. Theft of signing capability need not involve copying the key. The current “suspected to be copied” trigger misses that case. The draft provides neither a loss/theft classification rule nor bounded authenticated recovery if the key or every backup is lost. An author's later publication on captured channels cannot by itself repair distribution authenticity.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:108:These rules concern the release key. They do not add a service-key rotation project or a new receipt-verifier revocation rule.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:110:### C07 — MEDIUM: two publications made through the NUC do not establish authenticated bootstrap
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:112:**Locations:** :42, :46–50. Governing requirements: profile:399–400 and :1151–1158.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:114:Writing the fingerprint on paper is a useful local reference. Publishing the exported key through the NUC to the repository and website does not show that either publication still contains it, or how a new user authenticates the root. Under the stated capture, the NUC can substitute a different public key at both destinations. The fingerprint is not explicitly carried into the freeze record, documentation or relying-party text as the profile requires. This is a missing bootstrap procedure; it is not a claim that V1 must discover every targeted false download.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:116:**Exact fix:** export the ceremony public key, recompute its full fingerprint independently of the NUC, and compare it with the paper reference. Verify the actual bootstrap key and fingerprint placed in the freeze record, verifier documentation and relying-party text. State how a user obtains at least one authenticated initial fingerprint independently of the possibly captured distribution channels, or state authenticated initial acquisition as an explicit assumption with a concrete delivery method. Two matching web publications alone do not satisfy it. Retain the approved public bootstrap artifacts outside the NUC. Require existing trusted releases to authorize normal replacement keys, as C06 describes.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:134:| Repository credential, DNS credential and AWS account each attempt configuration authorization | No release authorization/signature; the genuine verifier refuses the unauthorized configuration, as the standing profile requires. |
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:168:No secret value is present in the inspected draft. It correctly forbids secrets in the document, keeps the AI from holding the release key/passphrase and asks the author not to disclose backup locations. Those are useful requirements. They do not yet specify safe handling of all PINs, reset material, temporary keys, logs and encrypted-key copies; C03–C04 supply that correction. The public custody record needs fingerprints, settings, procedure identity and check results, not passwords, recovery material or physical addresses.
docs/reviews/custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:172:Resolve the findings and obtain the author's concrete choices, then review the revised procedure and its non-secret evidence. Until then, do not use this draft to close release-key independence. If independence cannot be established before freeze, A10.4.2 requires the author-visible NARROW-MODEL route; removing the service-key list does not repair it. This review changes no instrument, profile, implementation or custody configuration.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:5:**Verdict: not ready to register.** The genuine V1-frame KMS capture remains incomplete, as the draft honestly reports. The selected slot-2 capture establishes HSM/signature compatibility but does not establish the deployed policy-signer path required by A9.6. The core rule-R encoding, external-μ equivalence, fingerprint definition and bounded P3 symbolic results withstand this review. Resolve the findings below before describing the pass-condition evidence as completed.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:11:**Locations:** supplement §§8, 10.3, 11.4; A9.6 pass condition; `hybrid-golden.json` SIG-H5/SIG-H6; `spike_v1_hybrid.py`.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:13:The real signatures are over 2123- and 6075-byte messages bearing `tessera-v1/receipt`, outside T. A and B correctly verify them as arbitrary pure ML-DSA messages; that does not turn those messages into `Frm(attest)`. R-H1/R-H2 and P-H1 use the public illustrative key. The prepared re-capture has not supplied the missing real-key evidence. This is an acknowledged completion blocker, not a discovered cryptographic failure. Owner decision 1 closes the proposed option of treating V1-shaped messages as sufficient.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:15:**Exact fix:** retain §10.3 as incomplete until the re-capture actually runs. Archive the genuine manifest, both attestation pairs, exact signatures and keys, the large frame's length and issuer-computed μ, capture hash, tool versions, and offline A/B verification output. Both frame decoders must accept the large signed frame as `attest`, and both verifiers must verify its exact KMS signature over that frame. Update §10.3 and the evidence hashes from that saved run, before 21 Oct. Do not re-frame SIG-H5/H6. Incorporate the new evidence into the frozen vector object as owner decision 6 requires. No cloud signing was performed in this review.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:17:### HS-02 — High: “as deployed” is not established by a direct GCP HSM capture
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:19:**Locations:** supplement §10.4 (“Met”), §11.5; A9.6 “Policy signer” and “Deadline”; `prototype/spike-pq/spike_gcp.py:49–55`; `spike_v1_hybrid.py:gcp_rule_o`.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:21:The selected GCP custody is consistent with A9.6, and SIG-H7 is a genuine verifying rule-O signature. But the capture calls `gcloud kms asymmetric-sign` directly from the instrument. The prepared re-capture does the same. Neither artifact exercises the queue-pulling policy signer, its complete-frame validation, its own-clock declared-time refusal, or the boundary preventing the issuing AWS account from invoking the GCP key directly. Changing the frame tag or pair id will not supply that missing evidence. This finding does not assert that deployment is absent elsewhere; the reviewed evidence does not identify it.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:**Exact fix:** change §10.4 to “HSM custody and offline rule-O compatibility met; deployed-slot path not yet evidenced.” For completion, identify the dated deployment/access-policy record and capture a signature through the selected external policy signer, with an accepted valid frame and a refusal for a declared time outside ε. Name the signer version, clock policy, queue path, key version and access boundary in the evidence record. Reuse already completed deployment evidence if it exists, with exact references; otherwise complete it by 21 Oct. Keep the offline rule-O checks. These are the existing A9.6 boundary and deadline, not an added independence-of-control claim.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:39:Both evidence implementations hard-code both ids in their candidate codec registries and use registry membership as the condition for signature verification. They have no selected catalog id or registration-state input. Consequently they verify the classical id even in a hypothetical hybrid installation, and they recognize the draft hybrid id even before registration. This is legitimate candidate-profile testing, but it cannot validate the supplement's claim that the unselected classical branch is policy-unsupported, or the profile's pre-registration opaque routing. XHR-09 only establishes the wholly unknown-id case. There is no newly discovered textual contradiction in the current profile: r2-F01 already resolves the earlier fork.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:41:**Exact fix:** label A/B results explicitly as candidate codec/cryptographic-profile evidence. Reference separate profile-conformance results with these cases: (a) before registration, hybrid id absent, nonempty opaque signatures accepted by the codec, semantics skipped, `ALG_UNKNOWN`; (b) after hybrid registration/selection, classical id still rule O, codec-valid input contributes `ALG_UNKNOWN` and skips key/signature/possession semantics; (c) codec-invalid classical signatures still produce INVALID; (d) wholly unknown consistent ids use opaque with `ALG_UNKNOWN`; (e) unequal frame/manifest alg values still fail receipt consistency independently of support. M5 must not add `SCHEMA` solely for an unsupported well-formed alg. Do not alter the shared pair id or the r2-F01 routing rule.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:47:A9.6 expressly requires slot key/fingerprint encodings and slot-appropriate key-use assumptions in its full-byte-profile pass condition, and expressly says baseline plus supplement governs. Keeping the ML-DSA SPKI definition and K-R in the supplement is therefore within the authorized branch completion; it need not amend E§3.3 or PROOF (K). The SHA-256/SPKI fingerprint scheme itself is unchanged. However §7 still offers consequential edits to E§8 and PROOF §6, and §11 still offers edits to E§3.3 and PROOF (K), outside the one baseline edit authorized here. DECISIONS.md has already selected leaving these definitions in the supplement. Likewise §1 still proposes RAW for small frames after the owner selected always EXTERNAL_MU.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:49:**Exact fix:** state that registration edits only E§5.2, with the branch-specific key definition and K-R carried by the supplement. Remove the optional additional baseline edits from the registration instructions; explain obsolete baseline “no vectors yet” statements as superseded for this branch by the supplement's evidence, without editing them under this authorization. Mark decisions 5–6 resolved and state always EXTERNAL_MU for attestation and possession. RAW captures may remain comparative evidence, clearly distinguished from the selected issuer path. The prepared instrument's RAW small/possession calls must not be described as testing that selected one-path issuer policy.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:57:**Exact fix:** cite RFC 9881 §§2/4 for the OID/SPKI and Appendix D for external μ. Label the §9 implementation-spec entry “design reference”; name the exact baseline object intended by A9.6's specification hash, and separately identify the frozen verification-spec object's hash at freeze. Name the final supplement and edited E§5.2 artifact versions in the registration record. Do not silently substitute one specification object for another.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:82:Python 3.14.6; ProVerif 2.05; rfc8785 0.1.4. Read the requested supplement, A9.6, E§3.3/§5, PROOF (K)/Lemma 7a and relevant proof boundaries, both hybrid implementations and vector sets, model headers/bodies/results and companion traces, owner decisions, current profile routing/M5, and the capture instruments used to assess completion.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:84:- A regenerated in a temporary copy, using the saved real captures: all self-checks passed; all four baseline/hybrid JSON files reproduced byte-for-byte. No source/vector file was regenerated in the repository.
docs/reviews/hybrid-supplement/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:The draft's hand-run external-library and planted-defect claims were not all rerun here. None of these checks establishes real ML-DSA/ECDSA security, historical key honesty, policy-signer deployment, full bundle validity, or capstone discharge. Registration remains conditional on the existing A9.6 deadline and completion evidence.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:13:| F01 — Publication does not guarantee visibility | APPLIED | A10:134–168 has the revised heading and conditional-publication boundary. It identifies both-channel capture, permits captor-created receipts under substituted keys, preserves publication as an issuer duty, and explicitly disclaims guaranteed publication, capture detection and public receipt visibility. The summary (:29–35) and row 16 agree. The author's quotation is retained expressly as motivation, not evidence (:146–151). |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:14:| F02 — Honest material before or after disclosure | APPLIED | The recommended clause (:155–159) protects honestly signed frames before or after credential disclosure while the original service keys and signing capability remain uncompromised. Options (a)–(d) distinguish this from a deliberate pre-capture restriction and from unsupported protection for arbitrary captor-created receipts (:174–179). The summary and row 15 agree. The backdating exclusion and guarantee 2's authority-capture proviso remain (:170–172). The conditional V2 obligation (:181–182) adds no V1 rule. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:16:| F04 — Complete clock correction inventory | APPLIED | Rows 5–9 contain the five requested source registrations and replacements; row 9 covers all three `before T` occurrences. Row 2 supplies an explicit replacement. A10.1 makes these qualifications operative (:70–95), preserves scenario i's party-signature duty and `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`, and leaves mapping verdicts, fixtures and case membership unchanged. The inherited A1.6 wording is expressly qualified for V1 (:93–95, :261–264). |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:17:| F05 — Precedence and independent checks | APPLIED | The paragraph before the stages (:192–196) preserves dependencies, dispatch, reason precedence and independently performable checks; UNVERIFIABLE is not an early return. Stage 2 retains merkle contradiction as INVALID. Stage 4, the cases and row 18 all preserve A4.2's failed-check precedence. Stage 3's additional sentence is consistent with the profile; see below. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:18:| F06 — Assembled C5 claim | APPLIED | A10:83–91 gives one assembled C5 claim cell that distinguishes the selected block, its header time T and the qualifying attestation. Rows 4 and 17 both point to that same replacement, avoiding conflicting overlapping edits. Existing non-claims remain, with the reorganization residual separately amended by row 14. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:20:| F08 — Plain-summary corrections | APPLIED | The opening is replaced (:16–17); Bitcoin, chain time and k are explained (:19–20); DNS is expanded at first use and the required owner is stated in code formatting (:38–39). The summary uses “service”, avoids “git” and the unexplained parameter/vector inventory, visibly attributes A02's source wording, and follows F01/F02's corrected capture scope. The corrected kinds are defined at :41–46. No style-guide edit is needed for these fixes. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:28:It also preserves **same-height aggregation**: a failing individual path does not defeat another satisfying qualifying path at exactly h*. The sentence prohibits moving to a higher height; it does not prohibit trying another path at the selected height. Thus, if qualifying receipt attestations exist at heights 100 and 200 but the txid subset can be satisfied only at 200, the receipt still fails `ANCHOR_TXID_MISMATCH` at 100. If another qualifying path at 100 satisfies that subset, the subset passes. Snapshot/git shape failures are similarly aggregated at their own h*, with `OTS_TX_SHAPE` if every qualifying path there fails. These are consequences of the existing profile, not newly executed vectors.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:30:The verdict/reason contract remains the profile's §15.7 (:1461–1465, :1497–1518): the first failed required check takes precedence over unperformability; existing dependencies and reason ordering remain. The addition introduces no new failure condition, reason code or verdict, and does not change any F01–F08 disposition above.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:38:| 1 | :36–37 | A10.1, opening clock claim | PASS |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:40:| 3 | :133 | A10.1, signed-frame clock claim | PASS |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:53:| 16 | :45–52, insertion | A10.4, new-receipt limitation and conditional publication; ruling pending | PASS |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:62:Repository HEAD during inspection: `44f921e2df2ea963eca7e32ab749349cc29c08ff`. A9 signing snapshot: `2ad34890359c446efaebcb116388b8e9ffbf6428`. Workspace A9 is byte-identical to that snapshot. Profile §7.1–§7.2 is also byte-identical between that snapshot and the inspected workspace, so the selection conclusion does not rely on the workspace's unrelated profile changes.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CONFIRMATION.md:71:Source-span matching, row structure/counts, kind letters and snapshot equality were checked mechanically; finding application, body/table correspondence and selection semantics were checked manually. This is textual confirmation only. No model, verifier implementation, cryptographic guarantee, publication enforcement or human test was newly run or discharged, and no new design review was undertaken beyond checking for HIGH issues.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:3:2026-10-13. Scope: `delta.diff` against the current proposed A9. Read A9 §A9.0, §A9.2, §A9.3 and §A9.6 in full, the opening claim/adversary/boundaries and C1–C7, ENCODING-V1 §3.3 and §5.2–5.4, and P8 PROOF's assumptions and relevant signature discussion. Searched `docs/` and `formal/` Markdown for `gpg`, `token`, `YubiKey` and `author-controlled`, and inspected the P8 codec/vector references. No implementation or provider deployment was tested. Only this review file is edited.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:13:The declared-time paragraph is unchanged: the second signer inspects the frame and compares `declared_issue_time` with its own clock, within ε. Its meaning remains issuer-side refusal, not a verifier check, proof of actual issue time, or lower time bound. But the new alternative specifies only key custody. A cloud KMS/HSM normally accepts a digest/signing request; it does not parse a V1 frame or apply this clock rule.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:15:Concrete allowed failure: run the queue consumer and OpenPGP adapter in the issuing AWS account, give it direct use-to-sign permission at the non-AWS provider, and retain an HSM-generated non-exportable key. An AWS takeover replaces the adapter, bypasses the clock check and requests signatures on backdated frames. Hardware location and non-exportability still meet the custody paragraph. The original queue-fed external signer protection no longer holds. This also exposes PROOF assumption (K): a generic signing endpoint does not itself restrict a key to V1 tagged type-0x00 documents. A token likewise needs host-side policy; hardware alone never enforced these conditions.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:21:> In either custody option, a queue-pulling policy signer outside the issuing AWS account validates the complete V1 frame and enforces the declared-time check using its own clock before requesting a signature. The issuing AWS account can enqueue frames but cannot invoke the slot-2 key directly, change that policy or clock, or obtain the policy signer's key-use credentials. The slot-2 key is dedicated to V1 tagged type-0x00 documents (attestation and manifest-possession). The dated custody record names this enforcement boundary and its access policy. Non-exportability protects against key export, not signing misuse through the permitted queue path.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:35:There is nevertheless a deadline ambiguity. The hybrid pass condition requires a real P-256 signature from the second slot **as deployed**, and the selected byte profile/key-use evidence, all by 21 Oct. Testing a token by 21 Oct and choosing an untested HSM adapter on 29 Oct does not meet that condition for the final deployed pair. The supplement cannot waive it: its authorization is bounded to the pair identifier/mode/context, the rule-R edit and completed pass evidence, “and nothing else”. Custody selection does not authorize changing rule O or silently extending the spike deadline. The fallback has no equivalent 21 Oct condition, but still needs the whole 30 Oct exit.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:39:> For the hybrid, the final custody implementation must have completed the deployed-slot pass-condition evidence by 21 Oct. The owner may defer selection until before 30 Oct only among custody implementations that each met that deadline; otherwise the registered fallback is taken. A custody selection changes neither rule O nor any claim, pass condition or exit obligation. Record the selected implementation and its evidence references, dated, in the profile before the freeze for either algorithm branch.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:47:ENCODING-V1's hybrid row still says “rule O (P-256, hardware token; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA)”. This is a normative table in A9's governing encoding baseline, not merely an old review quote. The new custody choice needs to be reconciled with it. The profile's fallback still requires gpg on author-controlled hardware. The runbook partially recognizes the new token branch at :25, yet unconditionally specifies YubiKey 1 at :11, calls the HSM migration “Path past V1”, titles §3 “Token-side”, and prescribes a token reset at exit. These instructions are inconsistent for an HSM selection.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:51:- ENCODING-V1:441: replace `rule O (P-256, hardware token; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA)` with `rule O (OpenPGP P-256; slot-2 custody per A9.6; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA)`.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:52:- V1-PROFILE:348–349: replace the fallback bullet with `- Fallback: both slots OpenPGP P-256 (rule O), KMS-backed for kms, and slot-2 custody per A9.6 for gpg. The dated pre-freeze custody record identifies the selected implementation.` Retain an updated source citation.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:54:- Replace the runbook's “Path past V1” bullet (:32–35) with `**Alternative V1 custody (A9.6):** an HSM-generated non-exportable P-256 key at a non-AWS provider, used through the external queue-pulling policy signer. The dated profile choice records the provider, key version, policy boundary, OpenPGP adapter and immutable public-key packet. Its authority is evidenced through the same DNSSEC and signed, anchored git key-publication record.`
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:57:- Derivation draft :47: replace `cloud HSM and local hardware token` with `AWS KMS and the selected slot-2 custody`; :64 and :250–251: replace the local-hardware sentences with `The gpg slot uses rule O under A9.6's selected slot-2 custody, through a queue-pulling policy signer outside the issuing AWS account.` Mark this as a dated reconciliation of a draft, preserving its decision history.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:A token on an external host and an HSM at a different provider can provide separate technical key-custody domains. Neither gives independent signing fates: the common AWS-fed queue can cause both keys to sign, both keys remain under one controller, and a P-256 break defeats both fallback slots. C3 explicitly retains these limits. A non-AWS provider name alone does not establish independence of the signer host, administration, IAM, clock or availability. Nor is end-to-end issuance availability independent of AWS when AWS supplies the queue.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:71:This preserves the defensible custody distinction and avoids reading it as a stronger guarantee. Finding 1 specifies the separate policy boundary necessary for the existing clock protection.
docs/reviews/a9-custody/CODEX-REVIEW.md:89:5. Current encoding/profile/runbook/draft inconsistencies are listed in finding 3. Historical documents are not silently rewritten. A9 remains PROPOSED in the inspected file; this review does not claim adoption, P8 discharge or deployment conformance.
docs/reviews/2026-09-16-codex-review-capstone-predictions.md:98:- **Link 1b as correspondence:** publication provenance is excluded from `ChainBroken`. (`:210–225`.)
docs/reviews/a9-custody/DISPOSITION.md:2:All four findings accepted. F1, F2 and F4: Codex's exact text applied to A9.6 (policy signer, deadline, limit; declared-time sentence; "separate technical key-custody domain"). F3: reconciliation of ENCODING-V1:441, V1-PROFILE, the runbook and the derivation draft delegated to a repair agent, using Codex's exact replacements; the derivation-draft change is marked as a dated reconciliation. Custody selected (owner, 2026-10-13): GCP Cloud KMS, HSM, P-256, in a dedicated project, with the policy signer in GCP. Recorded in the profile once the deployed-slot evidence exists, which must be by 21 Oct or the fallback is taken.
docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-kimi-review.md:33:The override in §A3.2.1 (boundary invariant replacing the digest-publication vs. authorized-tuple fork) is well-reasoned, but it leaves the design space very open.
docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-kimi-review.md:94:**Fatal errors: none.** The disposition is logically sound and consistent with the decoupled-clock architecture of A2.1. But I see one precision risk and one checkability gap that are worth tightening before the amendment is signed.
docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-kimi-review.md:104:Under decoupled clocks, block timestamps are non-monotonic, so conjunct 3 does **not** imply conjunct 2 (`anchor_time ≤ declared_issue_time + δ`). An issuer that ships on conjunct 3 alone could produce an honestly-shipped receipt that the verifier rejects — a divergence between issuance and verification that violates the correspondence A1.4 demands.
docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-kimi-review.md:124:**2. Add an explicit issuance conformance vector for the decoupled-clock divergence case**
docs/reviews/2026-08-08-A3-kimi-review.md:143:> *Formal rule statement incomplete under decoupled clocks; superseded by A3.7.3 — issuer must evaluate full P5 temporal conjunct set.*
