docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:28:A9.2 :205–210's formal/encoding exit items do not require the production release key or signing environment. They must still pass on their registered terms. Instrument TEST provisioning can support specification and conformance work; it cannot demonstrate the real release authority's implemented independence. Moving custody implementation does not move the formal exit, authorize early service code, or turn instrument results into production evidence.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:30:There **are existing documentary freeze prerequisites** affected by the move: profile §14.2 item 1 requires the named key and demonstrated boundary before 30 October; §5 puts its fingerprint in the freeze record; runbook §6 :115, :134–145 places custody, bootstrap tracking and a required refusal test at freeze. These are not a cryptographic reason to reject the move. They require an explicit, author-ratified change of timing. A10 :525–528 acknowledges the profile fingerprint/deadline change rather than pretending it was already permitted.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:32:At freeze, retain the release artifact/signature/preimage and trust-hash binding, verification format and dependency pinning required by profile §14.2 item 2. Also resolve the bootstrap/normal-rotation/bounded-recovery **rules** that item 2 already requires. Later ceremony and rehearsal supply their actual key, equipment and observed results. The adopted text and subsequent profile/runbook update should distinguish these registered rules from their later execution, and distinguish a pre-freeze TEST refusal vector from the later deployed-boundary rehearsal. A general postponement of item 1 must not silently postpone item 2's overlapping requirements.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:34:The independently retained freeze baseline also remains necessary. In particular, A10 :457–459 and row 32 still require the single service-key tuple to be authorized at freeze. That authorization can be preserved without an existing production release key, then checked and bound into the first release. The new gate does not authorize selecting a different tuple later from possibly captured channel assertions. The freeze's trust values, catalog and specification-source hashes remain the baseline, not inputs that the November NUC may redefine. The first verifier-code baseline is established independently when that code exists; the move does not require service code before its registered exit gate.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:46:### Fingerprint at first bootstrap publication
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:48:**Compatible with A9.5 and technically sound, but a change to the current profile.** Profile §5 :399–400 and runbook :134–136 currently require the fingerprint in the freeze record. Recording it later is not literal compliance with those sentences. A10 :525–528 openly proposes replacing that timing after the author's ruling. A9.5 requires the actual key and access boundary in a dated custody record; it does not require the key to predate specification freeze.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:72:Use the same archive identity through approval, signing, verification and publication. Add a rehearsal case with benign reviewed source and a malicious executable in an otherwise plausible candidate archive; it must be rejected or discarded and rebuilt before signing. This closes the remaining part of the original C01 attack and C05's actual-distributed-verifier requirement. It does not require reproducible builds if the independent build itself supplies the shipped archive.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:80:> A pre-gate TEST signature cannot authenticate a production release. Production authentication must use the independently authenticated production release key and must reject TEST authority, including TEST provisioning overrides. This does not protect a user who skips authentication or acquires a substituted initial key.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:84:> Each early release uses a separate test release key. Production checks never accept this key. Users must authenticate the production release key before they rely on a release.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:92:**Locations:** draft :232–247; A10 :525–528; profile :399–400; runbook :134–136.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:96:**Exact correction:** make the draft's step 3 inspect the dated first-production-release bootstrap record, verifier documentation and relying-party text. Require that later record to identify the original freeze baseline and actual production key, and require comparison with the ceremony reference. Preserve the original freeze record. After the author rules, reconcile profile §5, §14.2 items 1–2 and runbook §6 with the same registration/execution split described above. Do not edit those other files as part of this review.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:112:Explain ECDSA at first use and explain USB before or within its first use. Split the checker instruction into short rules for immutable fields/pins, horizon/version/hash linkage and permitted endpoint closure; preserve the profile's allowed null-to-end transition. Put rehearsal-record fields in a short list. Keep the corrected definitions, end references, mandatory wording and the conditional status of a later service window. No new release-count promise is needed.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:124:| **C07 — MEDIUM** | **WITH DEFECT** | §8 adds independent fingerprint computation, actual-publication checks, retained approved artifacts and an independent first-acquisition method/assumption. The freeze-record instruction conflicts with the new key-publication timing: R3-03. The concrete authenticated first-acquisition method remains a prerequisite, not something two matching web copies supply. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:132:The amendment's timing proposal preserves the central security boundary if adopted and implemented as described. The author's pending decisions remain pending. The quotation accepts risk (4) only. A10's rows 34–35 preserve independence under every capture-scope option and preserve the author-visible fallback; cutting the service-key listing still cannot cure a failed release boundary. Row 36 needs the scoped authentication conclusion and production-path check above.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r3/CODEX-REVIEW.md:136:No physical device, configured signing environment, backup, credential check, bootstrap delivery or rehearsal evidence was inspected. No release/key was generated, signed or restored; no implementation or formal/conformance suite was executed. The attack walkthroughs identify permitted readings of the procedure, not executed exploits. Only this review file was written; no amendment, disposition, profile, runbook or custody draft was edited.
