docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:16:“Every input” must mean every bundle/request given an installed, authenticated verifier and valid declared trust input. The profile deliberately specifies **no verdict** for an unauthenticated configuration or incompatible Unicode runtime. That is appropriate fail-closed startup behavior; it should not be advertised as a bundle verdict. Resource exhaustion or host failure is also not evidence that a bundle is INVALID.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:74:RFC 4035 §5.3.1 requires matching signer, algorithm and key tag, a usable Zone Key, and Labels constraints, among other checks. The profile says to perform that validation, but gives result classes only for missing evidence, potentially supported paths, time failure, crypto failure and DS mismatch. An intact TXT RRset with a supported RRSIG whose tag matches **no** DNSKEY is not a missing RRSIG, not a checked crypto failure, and not a child-DNSKEY/DS mismatch. Labels greater than the owner count, protocol/flag defects, and supported malformed key/signature encodings have similarly missing outcomes. “Unsupported ... could supply the path” does not define which mismatching/unauthenticated candidates count as potentially usable. One implementation can call an unmatched selector missing evidence, another INVALID, another mislabel it DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH. [RFC 4035 §5.3.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4035.html#section-5.3.1).
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:92:The profile promises that extra invalid candidates are informational after an eligible path passes, but also says **a TXT RRSIG** with fewer Labels causes DNS_WILDCARD, and lists that code among body checks before DNSSEC. A canonical snapshot offering a valid explicit-owner signature plus an extra less-Labels signature distinguishes “reject any offered wildcard” from “exclude wildcard candidates and accept the valid explicit witness.” Both are credible readings. Canonical group RRSIG ordering also has no final tie-breaker for signatures equal on tag/inception/expiration/signature bytes but differing in other RDATA; G35 fixes witness order, not wire group order.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:128:The two-byte hash prefix is outside the OpenPGP signed hash input. Mathematical ECDSA verification alone can therefore pass with an altered prefix, while a packet validator may reject. The profile has no explicit v4 prefix policy. Similarly a byte-shaped P-256 SPKI/key packet is not necessarily a valid public point; on-curve, finite and subgroup validation must not depend on library defaults. Git armor permits base64 characters but does not inherit the container's round-trip requirement, leaving nonzero-pad-bit aliases unspecified.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:162:**Exact fix:** choose a safe header grammar, for example ASCII repository-relative paths matching `[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+`, no empty/dot/dot-dot components, no leading slash, unique paths; length `0|[1-9][0-9]*`; fixed literal spaces and LF. Include only tracked regular file **blob bytes** from a named freeze commit; no newline conversion, symlink following, generated caches or working-tree overlay. Publish an exact sorted vector-path inventory in the freeze record, including non-P8 vectors, and an assembler plus independent byte agreement check. Put actual spec/vector/catalog hashes in a separate release/freeze record **outside the frozen spec parts**; otherwise recording them inside the included profile creates self-reference despite the stated no-cycle rule.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:182:IANA currently records KSK-2017 signing from **2018-10-11**, so the remembered **day** was correct. The proposed `2018-10-11T00:00:00Z` is a stronger claim: ICANN's operational account says the switch occurred at **16:00 UTC**. That does not by itself identify the inception of the first pre-generated root RRSIG; publication/switch time and RRSIG inception must remain distinct. Neither a remembered day nor a day-granularity IANA table proves midnight. [IANA key status](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/files), [ICANN operational account](https://www.icann.org/en/blogs/details/the-recent-ksk-rollover-summary-and-next-steps-30-10-2018-en).
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:188:### F16 — Medium: the adopted clock-check removal is not propagated to RES's text and vector obligations
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:192:P and RB correctly remove the clock comparison. RES's struck-out resolution/top table also records removal, but its quoted CORRECT text still says the verifier requires it; the issuer-duty paragraph still tells the issuer to check/wait for it; G31 still lists GIT_ASSERTION_AFTER_ISSUE; L-27/L-28 still test it, with L-28 requiring INVALID at declared + 1. P §14.2 imports all L-01–L-111 as freeze obligations. Thus the profile is correct locally yet imports a contradictory vector expectation. The user correction resolves authority: do **not** reinstate the clock check to satisfy the old vectors.
