docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CONFIRMATION.md:17:| F01 — empirical qualifications | **APPLIED** | A10:74–78 has the three requested plain evidence bullets. A10:272–289 retains the selected-sample, incomplete-window, observer-clock and quality-filter qualifications. Evidence:104–109 has the requested conditional interpretation; :115–117 replaces the “not censored” inference; :163–166 has the qualified conclusion. These passages no longer claim that the observations establish A-UTC. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:The mempool.space page was fetched directly and its 481-block count and −69/+82/41/40-second statistics match. Bowden et al. §II-B contains the cited header-order observations. Neither source supplies a production-clock bound. [mempool.space report](https://research.mempool.space/p/09defe3d-c836-44e7-b244-7f8434e72151), [Bowden et al.](https://arxiv.org/html/1801.07447v1#S2.SS2).
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:27:### F01 — MEDIUM: the empirical conclusions drop the censoring and clock qualifications
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:31:The measurements reproduce. The limitations are candidly recorded in the technical body, but the interpretation says A-UTC held and the plain summary says measurements found no six-block failure. Those are stronger statements than this dataset establishes. The dataset rejects arrivals outside ±7200 seconds of header time, and no lower bound on observer clock error is supplied. Taking the earliest observer arrival can select a slow clock. Propagation delay alone makes arrival lag conservative; an unbounded negative clock error does not.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:33:Writing an observer's recorded arrival as `a = r + propagation_delay + clock_error`, the inference `r − nTime ≤ a − nTime` needs `propagation_delay + clock_error ≥ 0`. Evidence :70 acknowledges that dependency. The interpretation and conclusion must retain it. The absence of retained observations between 3918 and 7200 seconds is descriptive evidence about this selected sample; it does not validate the excluded tail, the uncensored frequency of A-UTC failure, or an adversarial success probability. The sample also excludes windows with missing heights.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:37:> In every retained complete six-block window, at least one recorded arrival lag was at most 1064.9 s. The minimum computed U minus the inclusion block's recorded first-seen time was 6135.1 s; U's UTC interpretation remains conditional on A-UTC. If the selected observer readings do not understate production time, these observations support A-UTC for the sampled windows. Observer clock error is not bounded here. The dataset's ±7200 s quality filter and excluded incomplete windows prevent an inference about the frequency of A-UTC failures outside the retained sample.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:45:> [R] Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 remain a proposed trust tolerance. The retained observations show substantial recorded margins during ordinary operation, conditional on the observer-clock limitation. They do not validate the tolerance against an intentional timestamp coalition or establish a failure probability. Author acceptance of A-UTC remains necessary for the conditional UTC claim.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:51:> - Observer clocks can also be wrong. These measurements do not show that A-UTC was true.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:75:> Consensus requires each timestamp to exceed the median of the preceding eleven header times. The evidence reproduces 545 windows in which every recorded first-seen-minus-MTP value exceeds 7200 s. These MTPs use the actual predecessor timestamps and the observer-clock qualification. The count does not bound a coalition changing the six timestamps, since changes within the window also change later MTPs. A majority coalition can sustain lagging timestamps; A-UTC is not consensus protection against that adversary.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:92:The six-header formula gives `1443237574` at h = 376155 and `1443237512` at h = 376156: moving one height later lowers the reported value by 62 seconds. These are formula evaluations, not evidence that one real receipt has both attestations. They refute the claimed monotonic reason for excluding favourable selection; the profile permits presentation-sensitive selection.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:106:**Locations:** A10 :101–103; compare :450–458, A9 :326–332, profile §5 :389–407 and §14.2 item 1.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:108:“Nobody with the DNS credential, the repository key or the AWS account can use the release key” is stronger than the registered rule. It prevents the same authorized person from holding operational credentials and separately authorizing a release. The technical requirement is that capture of those credentials/accounts confers no release-signing or release-authorization capability. The project retains one controller; technical access separation does not establish separate human control. This is a STE-STYLE faithfulness finding, not a request to change custody architecture.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:114:> - The DNS credential and the repository key must not give access to the release key.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:115:> - Access to the AWS account must not give access to the release key.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:116:> - Hardware or offline software for the release key is acceptable only with a written procedure.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:126:For example, let production times be `100000 + 600i` and timestamps `92700 + 600i`, i = 0…5. All six lags are 7300 seconds; the formula still gives `102900`, later than the inclusion production time `100000`. Also, six lagging timestamps need not imply that an attacker controlled all six miners: clock faults or coincident behaviour can satisfy the same failure condition.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:204:**Custody gate.** At the preregistration level it is concrete enough to check: the existing profile requires the actual key, holder and implemented access boundary shown unusable by the three operational credentials/accounts; A10 adds a written authorization/signing/backup procedure, a dated custody-record reference and review before freeze. Hardware purchase alone is explicitly insufficient. Profile §14.2's bootstrap, rotation and bounded recovery requirements remain standing. Nothing reviewed supplies the actual custody evidence or closes independence. A completed procedure is not itself proof of independence. If independence fails, the author must receive the affected NARROW-MODEL; cutting the list does not repair the old trust boundary or authorize silently freezing a reduced claim.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r2/CODEX-REVIEW.md:212:No issuer/verifier implementation, conformance vector or formal-model run was added or executed. The reproduced observational numbers, arithmetic examples and finite classifier exercise do not establish A-UTC, deployed custody, empirical adversarial security or public capture visibility. Prospective vectors and profile updates remain prospective work following any author ruling.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:29:For example, after theft the attacker can genuinely stamp a new first-listing configuration at current height H. The attacker then supplies fabricated receipt headers at H+6 through H+11, pins their hashes in the forged release chain, and sets their header times inside the old issuance window. A counterfeit old-window receipt and its OTS path can be constructed against those fabricated merkle roots. The listing's genuine H through H+5 pins pass the stated independent check, and H+5 < H+6 passes. V1 deliberately performs no PoW, nBits or retarget validation: equality with a supplied pin substitutes for those checks. Corresponding false authority-history pins/trust entries can support the counterfeit DNS/git evidence in a fork from version 1. This attack needs no change to an intact honest receipt's signed bytes; it supplies a new counterfeit for the old window under the thief's keys.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:35:> The retrospective protection requires a conforming verifier and independently authenticated Bitcoin history for both selected anchors and their six-header segments. The receipt, listing, snapshot and git proofs must use the same independently authenticated history. For this theft protection, a signature under the possibly stolen release key is not sufficient pin authentication. Compare every used pin with independently validated Bitcoin history, or with a previously retained, independently authenticated genuine release that covers that height. An earlier release with no pin at the height supplies no witness. A party unable to establish this history has not established the retrospective protection. Authenticating only the bootstrap public-key fingerprint does not distinguish a fork signed with the stolen genuine key. An independently retained genuine release/configuration commitment can distinguish such a fork to the extent of its recorded contents and ancestry.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:82:“Under the registered failure precedence” preserves INVALID over UNVERIFIABLE, but does not insert new checks into the profile's **total reason order**. That table has no listing rows. For identical inputs failing both listing order and issuance-window checks, one implementation could report `LISTING_NOT_PRECOMMITTED`, another `ISSUANCE_WINDOW`. Both obey verdict-class precedence while disagreeing on a stable field.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:103:Limit :483's append-stability assertion explicitly to the original **tuple classifier**. Do not extend it to the height comparison: pin extensions can qualify a previously nonqualifying earlier attestation and change the selected height. Preserve A9.4's exact-output promise for identical complete declared inputs, and the separation of historical-security assessments. Update row 37 and the mechanism accounting for the selected complete error taxonomy; the concrete correction above adds `LISTING_STAMP_MISMATCH`, making four new reason codes for A10.4.1 rather than three. Make these profile changes after the author's ruling, as A10 already requires.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:107:**Inequality and commitment target.** The strict comparison has the same off-by-one convention as existing C4: confirmation six is h_release+5, and equality with h_receipt must fail. Stamping the full configuration hash binds the exact domain, both fingerprints and both window endpoints; stamping only a release name/version or a mutable pointer would not. Under authentic shared history and the continuing hash/history assumptions, a thief cannot backdate a newly constructed configuration into a fixed, genuinely past receipt anchor. This establishes commitment ordering, not UTC, first publication, or general safety after distribution compromise.
docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/CODEX-REVIEW.md:113:Only this review file was written. No amendment, profile, custody procedure, model or prior review was edited.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:3:2026-10-16. **Result: revise before signing.** A10 correctly identifies the six source issues, and its substantive A03 counting argument matches the executable P5c guard. It still overstates capture visibility, unnecessarily excludes honest material signed after credential disclosure, leaves material clock wording unreconciled, mislabels three narrowing rows, and needs a verdict-precedence qualification. These are document findings, not demonstrated implementation failures.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:11:Read A10 in full; all A01–A06 findings in `docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md`; A9's opening, claim table, scenario mapping, and every passage A10 changes or invokes; A8 §A8.3/§A8.5; A1 §A1.1, P5 and §A1.6; A2 §A2.1's clock roles and convention pin; the cited encoding owner-name rules; and the profile's qualification, selection, pin and precedence rules. Also read the complete revision-2 pilot and `docs/plain/STE-STYLE.md`.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:Locations below use these inspected files. A9 locations and the encoding locations explicitly marked “signing snapshot” were checked with `git show 2ad3489:<path>`, rather than inferred from current line numbers. The cited profile §7.1–§7.2 rules were also present at that commit; the workspace's unrelated profile/encoding changes do not supply new authority for them.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:25:## F01 — HIGH: publication duty does not guarantee visibility after capture
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:29:The proposed boundary says “Such capture is made visible” and the summary says “The public git repository makes those new receipts visible.” A9 registers public publication as an **issuer duty**, explicitly outside what C4 proves. That does not force a credential thief to publish its substitute assertion. Nor does publishing a key assertion publish every receipt made under it. The proposed text turns an operating duty and an opportunity to notice a substituted key into an unconditional adversarial guarantee.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:31:A concrete construction permitted by the stated checks is a captor with both channel credentials making matching DNS evidence and a privately retained, signed git assertion for its own service-slot keys. It anchors the assertion, waits for the registered chain-order gap, and anchors a receipt signed by those keys. The public repository need not contain the private assertion. Assuming eligible trust intervals, supported algorithms and the remaining checks, publication is no verifier obstacle. This is a construction argument from the rules, **not an executed V1 forgery vector**. Capture of only one required channel does not generally suffice.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:35:> V1 does not prevent a captor of both required authority-channel credentials from issuing new receipts under a key assertion that names the captor's own keys, even while the original service keys remain uncompromised. Public publication of the key-assertion repository remains an issuer duty (A9.7, “Changed in effect”). If the substituted assertion is publicly published, a third party can inspect the substituted keys. The verifier does not check publication; V1 does not guarantee that a captor publishes the assertion, that capture is detected, or that new receipts are publicly visible.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:37:Change A10.4's heading to `Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty`. Replace the summary's A04 visibility sentences with:
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:39:> The service must publish its authority record. A third party can inspect a substituted key if that record is public. The verifier does not check publication. V1 does not guarantee that an attacker publishes the record.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:43:## F02 — MEDIUM: “before the capture” gives up more than the tested property requires
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:47:A8.3 protects **honest frames, signer sets and signatures** under credential disclosure; it does not restrict these to receipts issued before disclosure. The distinction is honest service material versus captor-created material, not simply old versus new receipts. In the actual `capstone_d.pv` fixture, :1121 publishes the authority secret before the replicated honest issuer processes at :1128–1130 run. In strict both-channel-capture companion `cap_cc01_c.pv`, :1148 publishes both secrets before the honest signing processes at :1156–1158. These fixtures do not model a pre-capture signing phase.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:49:The before-capture restriction is explicitly labelled NARROW-CLAIM, so it is not concealed. It is nevertheless an additional reduction that A04's source finding does not require, and the options do not distinguish retaining protection for honest post-capture frames from promising presentation for arbitrary captor-created receipts. The latter has no blocking invariant; that does not imply the former must be abandoned. This observation does not promote slice reds to proof of all correct-form properties or to V1 implementation evidence.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:53:> anyone who captures authority-channel credentials (the DNS credential or the repository key, including both together) while the original service keys stay secret and their signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture), as to frames honestly signed by those service keys, whether before or after credential disclosure: such a captor cannot reattribute those frames to other keys or another service, alter their honest signer sets, or carry their signatures onto other bytes or keys (A8.3);
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:57:> (a) Adopt the bounded honest-material claim above, with the new-receipt limitation and conditional publication statement. (b) Deliberately restrict even that claim to receipts honestly issued before capture; this gives up the honest post-capture material covered by A8.3's stated property and must be recorded as an additional author choice. (c) Extend presentation protection to arbitrary new receipts under captor-selected keys; this needs a named blocking invariant that V1 does not have, and is not supported by this amendment. (d) Rule other text. Rows 10–11 take effect only on the author's ruling.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:59:Update the summary and heading consistently. Keep the explicit statement that A8.3 does not test backdating, and keep guarantee 2's authority-capture proviso. Nothing here requires adding a mechanism to rescue option (c).
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:65:Rows 8–9 remove the exactly-k reorganization case from A9's literally claimed protection. That is a claim reduction even though it faithfully corrects the boundary to match the model. A10 itself acknowledges this at :47–48. Calling it CORRECT does not satisfy the requested distinction. Row 4 applies the same removal of a UTC interpretation to the success definition that rows 1–3 correctly label NARROW-CLAIM; leaving the sentence's bytes unchanged does not leave its claimed meaning unchanged.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:77:## F04 — MEDIUM: the clock correction omits registered uses of the same unsupported bound
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:102:A1.6:485–505 contains older compressed clock/depth language too. A10 explicitly leaves A1 standing. State that this is a V1 qualification of the inherited clock wording, rather than claiming that unchanged historical prose itself proves a UTC bound. No model equation needs to change to make this documentary boundary honest.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:106:**Location:** A10:148–165, :196. Source: A9:287 (A4.2 precedence); profile:789–802, :818–834, :1497–1509.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:108:The qualification and lowest-height selection conditions match the cited profile, including the covered h=100 plus headerless h=90 example. However, row 13 says “if no attestation qualifies, or a supplied header is unpinned, the result is UNVERIFIABLE.” That is too strong. A sole attestation contradicted by its pinned header fails `OTS_MERKLE_MISMATCH` and leaves no qualifying attestation. Stage 2 correctly identifies INVALID; row 13 then prescribes UNVERIFIABLE. Similarly, an unpinned header can coexist with another independently performable failed required check. A4.2 still requires INVALID to win.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:110:The stages also need to be identified as selection logic, not a new early-return verification procedure. The profile independently checks all supplied consecutive-height links (`HEADER_CHAIN_BROKEN`), OTS grammar and stamp digests, and selected-height transaction/path requirements. Failure to qualify must not suppress those checks or let a verifier jump to a higher height to evade a failed selected-path check.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:132:Keep the existing non-claims, with the reorganization residual amended separately. This gives the body and table a single reviewable replacement and retains the original selection provenance.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:158:- Preserve the distinction between NARROW-CLAIM and the other kinds with F03's corrected definition. Revise the A04 summary to protect honest signed material under F02, or explicitly identify the author's additional before-capture restriction if chosen.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:160:These additions use the supplied dictionary and leave `STE-STYLE.md` untouched. They do not certify all of that dictionary's proposed entries for publication.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:166:| A01 | A9:36–37, :114, :133, :144, :294–295, :433–446; A2.1 clock roles | Existence before inclusion is supported under the commitment/history assumptions; a finite UTC upper bound is not. Direction correct; complete the inventory and assembled text under F03, F04, F06. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:169:| A04 | A8.3:72–77; A8.5:123–124; capture fixtures; A9 publication duty and trust rules | Honest-material binding and arbitrary new issuance are distinct. The recommendation needs F01/F02; the author still decides. |
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:182:Consequently with k=6 and DepthK=5, a five-confirmation anchor has depth=4 and can be orphaned; a six-confirmation anchor has depth=5 and cannot. Removing six old tip blocks at that moment would remove the anchor, and is outside this model's transition relation. The model has no separate variable for the number of old blocks a reorganization removes: A10 defines that ordinary-language quantity and relates it to the guarded anchor depth.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:184:Both `_Broken.tla`:65–70 and `_BrokenSilent.tla`:73–78 retain the same strict reorganization guard; their mutations do not test orphaning an anchor at the permanence threshold. The default P5c config uses DepthK=2, so it is not itself a six-confirmation instantiation. The actual `k6-2026-09-14/P5c/P5c_k6_d3.cfg` and `P5c_k6_d6.cfg` both use DepthK=5. The archived d6 output reports completion without an error; that is an inspected historical result, not a fresh run. The bridge explicitly omits reorgs (`P5cP5P6_Bridge.tla`:65–69). None of these files supplies equality-case orphaning coverage.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:186:This supports A10's “never covered” statement **for the cited P5c/bridge model family and registered permanence assumption**. It is not a proof that Bitcoin cannot reorganize by six blocks. Some model comments use “depth k” loosely; the executable guards and A2's explicit mapping are the load-bearing evidence.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:190:No mechanism, formal action, cryptographic algorithm, tolerance, k/N/S value, frame, `A_sig` encoding, authority-record grammar, vector or registered verdict mapping is newly introduced by the proposed corrections. The selection conditions and reason codes in A10.5 already exist in the signing-snapshot profile. F05 identifies a contradictory prose verdict instruction that prevents an unconditional “no verdict change” pass as drafted; its fix preserves the existing rule. F04 changes the explanation of registered safe decisions without changing their outcomes. No additional model run or vector is required to correct these documentary defects.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:192:The current table is not ready to sign as a complete inventory: all fourteen existing locators/quotations match, but F04 adds missing registrations, F03 changes kinds, and F06 supplies the assembled replacement. Rows 10–11 honestly remain conditional on the author's ruling. The final table and summary must be regenerated from the author's chosen A04 text and the completed clock inventory.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:196:**No remaining ADDS, DROPS or CHANGES found within its stated scope:** A9's opening claim/adversary/boundaries and C1–C7, with the source rules those clauses invoke. Rechecked the restored assumptions, issuance-event wording, both signature frames, C3 control/misuse limits, complete git path and six-confirmation chain-order gap, temporal comparisons, all-supplied-header rule, C6 limits, C7's bounded human-test criterion, and missing-status-capture negative control. The earlier F01–F23/O01–O05 omissions and changes are repaired in this revision.
docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md:202:Source-span/line matching and input hashes were checked mechanically; claim boundaries, style and proposed fixes were reviewed manually. Actual model source and archived run output were inspected. No ProVerif/TLC model, V1 implementation, cryptographic assumption, publication enforcement, or human test was newly executed or discharged by this review. No signed source, pilot, style guide, profile, encoding or model was edited.

