   107	The spec, conformance-vector and preamble objects are content-addressed: their SHA-256 values sit inside the
   108	signed common bytes (§3.2), and they are never signed or anchored on their own.
   109	
   110	**Where each of the seven symbolic bindings lives** (A5 §A5.4; A7 §A7.9; R-3.12a):
   111	
   112	| Binding (`framed(...)` argument) | Lives in | Equal across slots? |
   113	|---|---|---|
   114	| object type `objType` | the envelope's `type_tag` = `tessera-v1/attest` | yes (one tag for both slots) |
   115	| canonicalization version `canonVer` | the envelope's `canonicalization_version` = `0x0001` | yes |
   116	| algorithm `alg` | the payload's `common.alg` (A3 §A3.6.1: in the canonical payload) | yes |
   117	| issuer identity `issuerId` | the payload's `common.issuer` = D | yes |
   118	| manifest hash `manifestHash` | the payload's `common.manifest_sha256` = hex(SHA256(manifest frame)) | yes |
   119	| payload | the payload's `common` member: the requester's `content` plus the common metadata | yes, **byte-equal** |
   120	| key fingerprint `kfp` | the payload's `signer.key_fp`, the **only** signer-specific member | no |
   121	
   122	**Requester content versus signer metadata.** The requester's content is exactly `common.content`. All other
   123	`common` members are common metadata, written by the issuer and equal for both signers. `signer` holds the only
   124	signer-specific field. That makes A5 §A5.4's "payload equal across signer slots" a statement about bytes (§3.4).
   125	
   126	---
   127	
   128	## 1. Canonical payload bytes: RFC 8785 over the accepted domain
   129	
   130	### 1.1 The accepted value domain V
   131	
   132	A JSON value is in V if and only if all of the following hold.
   133	- **Literals.** `null`, `true` and `false`.
   134	- **Numbers.** A finite IEEE 754 binary64 value other than −0. *Reason: this is exactly what RFC 8785 can
   135	  serialize without loss.*
   136	- **Strings, including member names.** A sequence of Unicode scalar values with:
   137	  - no surrogate code point (U+D800 to U+DFFF), and so no lone `\uD8xx` escape;
   138	  - no noncharacter (U+FDD0 to U+FDEF, and U+xFFFE or U+xFFFF in every plane). *Reason: RFC 7493 §2.1 (I-JSON); G-04.*
   139	  - no code point that is unassigned (General_Category `Cn`) in **Unicode 16.0.0**. *Reason: this pins the NFC test
   205	  lowercase hex otherwise. Every other scalar appears literally.
   206	
   207	*Reason: R-3.1 binds to RFC 8785. Vectors C-04 and C-05 pin the escape and the UTF-16 ordering traps, for example
   208	U+1F600 sorting before U+E000.*
   209	
   210	### 1.4 Decoding, and the order in which rejections are reported
   211	
   212	Two entry points.
   213	- `ingest(text)` takes requester JSON: whitespace is allowed, and the depth limit is 62.
   214	- `payload(bytes)` takes in-frame bytes: the depth limit is 64, and the bytes must be canonical. Before the
   215	  checks below it enforces §1.1's size limit: more than 2^24 bytes gives `LENGTH_CAP`, the code a frame's
   216	  `payload_length` check gives for the same bytes (§3.1), so the standalone entry point and the frame agree.
   217	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, P8F-04]
   218	
   219	Each runs these checks in this order and reports the **first category that fails**, scanning the whole value for
   220	each category. *Reason: both implementations then agree on the reason code, not only on rejection.*
   221	
   222	1. `JSON_ENCODING`: the bytes are not strict UTF-8. Overlongs, encoded surrogates and values above U+10FFFF are
   223	   rejected.
   224	2. `JSON_SYNTAX`:
   225	   - a BOM; not exactly one RFC 8259 JSON text; trailing non-whitespace;
   226	   - a raw control character in a string; bad escapes; leading zeros; `+`; `.5`; `1.`; `NaN`; `Infinity`.
   227	3. `JSON_DEPTH`.
   228	4. `JSON_DUPLICATE_NAME`.
   229	5. `JSON_STRING`: a surrogate, a noncharacter, an unassigned code point, or text that is not NFC.
   230	6. `JSON_NUMBER`: §1.2.
   231	7. *(`payload` only)* `JSON_NONCANONICAL`: the bytes are not equal to `JCS` of the parsed value.
   232	
   233	---
   234	
   235	## 2. The tagged byte strings: one header, four tags
   236	
   237	Every byte string that a V1 slot key signs (attestation and manifest frames), and every receipt-anchor and snapshot
   238	preimage that OTS stamps, begins with the same header. Two V1 byte strings carry no header: the git channel's
   239	stamped message, which is exactly the 40 lowercase hex digits of the signed commit id followed by one LF (A9.5,
   240	§7.2), and the key-publication value (§7), which the repository key and the DNSSEC keys sign through git and DNS.
   241	Neither is a frame (Remark 10 of PROOF.md). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C6] *(Was: "Every V1 byte string that a V1
   242	key signs or that OTS stamps begins with the same header:", which the git stamped message contradicts.)*
   243	
   244	The header:
   245	
   246	```
   247	header(t) = u8 len(t) || t                       t ∈ T (ASCII, no NUL)
   248	```
   249	
   250	| Tag `t` | len | First bytes (hex) | Construction |
   251	|---|---|---|---|
   252	| `tessera-v1/attest` | 17 | `11 74 65 73 …` | frame, §3 |
   253	| `tessera-v1/manifest` | 19 | `13 74 65 73 …` | frame, §4 |
   254	| `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig` | 29 | `1d 74 65 73 …` | signature-inclusive anchor preimage `A_sig`, §5 |
   255	| `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot` | 26 | `1a 74 65 73 …` | snapshot preimage, §6 |
   256	
   257	`tessera-v1/receipt-anchor` (25, `19 …`), the frame-only anchor tag of the 2026-10-09 draft, is **withdrawn**. It
   258	is not in T, so it is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (§5.1).
   259	
   260	- One string serves as both the domain-separation tag and the object type. R-3.11 lists the two as separate items,
   261	  but A1 P8's four-field frame has a single slot. The prefix `tessera-v1/` does the domain separation, and the
   262	  suffix names the type (G-07 resolved).
   263	- A tag outside T is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN`. That includes P7 types V1 does not use (`tessera-v1/wrapper`)
   264	  and later versions (`tessera-v2/attest`). A tag in T that is not the expected one is rejected with
   265	  `TAG_MISMATCH`. *Reason: these are the registered "type-tag mismatch" rejections, and the tag decides the
   266	  construction before any other byte is read.*
   267	- The length byte makes T prefix-free as headers. The proof's Lemma 4 depends on that.
   268	- *Why ASCII tags:* a reader with no V1 software can see them in a hex dump. That helps C6.
   269	
   270	---
